WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.140 --> 00:00:12.860
- So thank you so much for joining us

00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:14.810
for Climate Change &amp; Global Development:

00:00:14.810 --> 00:00:17.256
Net-Zero after COVID-19.

00:00:17.256 --> 00:00:19.380
This is the seventh in a series

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:21.160
of Yale's Development Dialogues

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:22.990
which are virtual panel discussions

00:00:22.990 --> 00:00:24.840
hosted by the Yale Economic Growth Center,

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:26.540
the South Asian Studies Council

00:00:26.540 --> 00:00:28.880
and the Jackson Institute
for Global Affairs.

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:31.600
And as you may know, if
you've joined us in the past,

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:33.880
we look to lessons from
history and economics

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:35.900
to address today's policy problems

00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:38.000
in low and middle-income countries.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:38.890
I'm Catherine Cheney,

00:00:38.890 --> 00:00:40.770
I'm a journalist focused
on global development

00:00:40.770 --> 00:00:42.510
and I'll be your moderator today.

00:00:42.510 --> 00:00:45.840
And I'm joined by panelists
including Sir Dieter Helm,

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:47.490
who's professor of economic policy

00:00:47.490 --> 00:00:49.030
at the University of Oxford,

00:00:49.030 --> 00:00:50.370
and former independent chair

00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:53.420
of the UK Natural Capital
Committee which provides advice

00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:56.540
to the government on the
sustainable use of natural capital.

00:00:56.540 --> 00:00:59.840
Plus, you'll hear from
Rohini Pande, Sunil Amrith

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:02.050
and Rory Stewart who are organizers

00:01:02.050 --> 00:01:03.330
and co-hosts of this series

00:01:03.330 --> 00:01:05.300
and I'll introduce them in just a moment.

00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:07.019
But first just to set up the topic,

00:01:07.019 --> 00:01:10.110
the topic of green growth
has come up a few times

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:12.400
over the course of the
Yale Development Dialogues

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:14.180
and there's been some debate around it

00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:16.010
but we haven't really tackled this head on

00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:17.860
and that's what we'll be doing today.

00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:19.410
So in climate negotiations,

00:01:19.410 --> 00:01:21.470
low-income countries have long asserted

00:01:21.470 --> 00:01:24.268
that they are being asked to
sacrifice economic development

00:01:24.268 --> 00:01:26.570
for a problem that's largely caused

00:01:26.570 --> 00:01:28.500
by high-income countries.

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:31.242
And today, western countries
set bold emissions targets

00:01:31.242 --> 00:01:34.390
even as they continue to
import goods manufactured

00:01:34.390 --> 00:01:36.000
in low and middle income countries

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:38.160
at a high environmental cost.

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:40.840
So countries like India
may in fact increase

00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:42.030
their reliance on coal

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:44.160
to help mitigate the economic devastation

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:45.900
caused by the coronavirus pandemic,

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:48.919
which of course has really
become a crisis in India today

00:01:48.919 --> 00:01:52.790
even as they set targets for
renewable energy production.

00:01:52.790 --> 00:01:54.690
I should note that this
dialogue is being held

00:01:54.690 --> 00:01:58.430
on Yale Climate Day which is
a full day of virtual events.

00:01:58.430 --> 00:02:00.360
This provides an opportunity
for the Yale community

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:01.590
and the public to come together

00:02:01.590 --> 00:02:03.820
around pressing environmental challenges.

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:05.760
And you can click the link in the chat

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:07.030
if you're joining us on Zoom

00:02:07.030 --> 00:02:09.160
for details on other events today

00:02:09.160 --> 00:02:12.340
including John Kerry's
keynote this afternoon.

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:14.670
As I mentioned as people
were still signing on,

00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:16.670
for those of you joining us here on Zoom,

00:02:16.670 --> 00:02:18.340
please do submit your questions throughout

00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:20.890
using the chat or Q&amp;A function.

00:02:20.890 --> 00:02:23.040
And now I wanna go ahead
and introduce our panelists

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:23.980
and move into discussion.

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:26.830
So earlier I mentioned Dieter Helm.

00:02:26.830 --> 00:02:28.027
His most recent book,

00:02:28.027 --> 00:02:30.670
"Net Zero: How We Stop
Causing Climate Change"

00:02:30.670 --> 00:02:32.560
addresses the actions we all need to take

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:34.030
to tackle the climate emergency

00:02:34.030 --> 00:02:36.380
and we'll hear more from him on that.

00:02:36.380 --> 00:02:39.440
Sunil Amrith is the Renu
and Anand Dhawan professor

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:40.690
of history and current chair

00:02:40.690 --> 00:02:43.410
of the South Asian
Studies Counsel at Yale.

00:02:43.410 --> 00:02:46.310
His research focuses on histories
of environment, migration

00:02:46.310 --> 00:02:49.000
and public health across
South and Southeast Asia.

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:51.520
Rohini Pande is the Henry J. Heinz

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:53.060
the second professor of economics

00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:55.530
and director of the Economic
Growth Center at Yale.

00:02:55.530 --> 00:02:58.090
Her research has examined
the political economy of aid

00:02:58.090 --> 00:03:01.200
and empowerment of the
in developing countries.

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:03.550
And Rory Stewart is a
former member of parliament

00:03:03.550 --> 00:03:04.850
and secretary of state

00:03:04.850 --> 00:03:07.280
for international development in the UK.

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:09.407
He's written extensively
about travel, politics

00:03:09.407 --> 00:03:11.880
and development and
argued against the merging

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:13.420
of the Department for
International Development

00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:16.720
and the Foreign Office
now known as the FCDO,

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:18.320
you may be familiar.

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:20.870
So I wanna begin with a
question for each of you

00:03:20.870 --> 00:03:23.760
just to get your take on the
topic we're addressing today

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:25.870
and then we can dive into details

00:03:25.870 --> 00:03:27.880
of what you have to say and
get into a conversation.

00:03:27.880 --> 00:03:31.510
But I think we all agree.

00:03:31.510 --> 00:03:33.390
We might not agree on the path to this

00:03:33.390 --> 00:03:34.850
but I think we all agree

00:03:34.850 --> 00:03:39.120
that the traditional methods
of growth must change

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:42.130
in order to address climate
and development goals.

00:03:42.130 --> 00:03:43.700
So the question is, and this is where

00:03:43.700 --> 00:03:45.750
there might be some
disagreement and debate,

00:03:45.750 --> 00:03:48.750
what are some of the hard
decisions that need to be made

00:03:48.750 --> 00:03:50.920
and who needs to step up?

00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:53.000
So just to get your take on this topic,

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:56.090
let's begin with Dieter,
if that's all right.

00:03:56.090 --> 00:03:57.110
- Well, thank you very much

00:03:57.110 --> 00:03:57.943
and thank you very much

00:03:57.943 --> 00:04:00.240
for asking me along to this discussion.

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:04.410
So almost all discussions
about environmental concerns

00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:08.490
start with the question of the
economy and economic growth.

00:04:08.490 --> 00:04:12.700
And the alternative is put
forward as either green growth

00:04:12.700 --> 00:04:14.260
or no growth, club of our own thought

00:04:14.260 --> 00:04:17.070
that we should have no growth at all.

00:04:17.070 --> 00:04:21.718
My take on this is that
growth is perfectly plausible

00:04:21.718 --> 00:04:25.499
provided it's driven
by technology and ideas

00:04:25.499 --> 00:04:28.670
and that the scope for the advancement

00:04:28.670 --> 00:04:32.020
of human ingenuity of
technology is enormous.

00:04:32.020 --> 00:04:37.020
However, we can only consume
at a level which is consistent

00:04:37.070 --> 00:04:39.900
with sustaining those
opportunities through time.

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:42.610
So the fundamental
environmental observation

00:04:42.610 --> 00:04:44.940
to kick off with is we are all living

00:04:44.940 --> 00:04:47.573
beyond our sustainable
environmental means.

00:04:48.630 --> 00:04:51.370
In other words, we are consuming too much

00:04:51.370 --> 00:04:54.480
and the level of our
consumption has to adjust down

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:57.730
before it can continue
to go up into the future.

00:04:57.730 --> 00:04:58.930
And why is that?

00:04:58.930 --> 00:05:02.810
It's because you and me are
ultimately the polluters

00:05:02.810 --> 00:05:05.650
and we're not paying the
cost of our pollution

00:05:05.650 --> 00:05:08.480
including carbon emissions and the like.

00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:11.850
And whether those emissions
are caused in India or China

00:05:11.850 --> 00:05:16.010
or Africa or they're caused in London

00:05:16.010 --> 00:05:18.780
is irrelevant in the
climate change example.

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:23.510
So we have to step up and pay
the costs of our pollution

00:05:23.510 --> 00:05:26.840
and recognize that we're
responsible for far more pollution

00:05:26.840 --> 00:05:28.150
than we're currently causing.

00:05:28.150 --> 00:05:30.450
And that leads to some
pretty hard decisions

00:05:30.450 --> 00:05:33.290
because of course,
politicians around the world

00:05:33.290 --> 00:05:38.290
from Biden to Johnson, to
Von der Leyen in the EU

00:05:38.670 --> 00:05:41.330
are all promising to boost consumption

00:05:41.330 --> 00:05:44.240
to solve our current economic difficulties

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:45.610
from the coronavirus.

00:05:45.610 --> 00:05:47.967
And simply boosting
consumption is not a model

00:05:47.967 --> 00:05:50.330
which is going to deliver

00:05:50.330 --> 00:05:53.040
the kinds of environmental
adjustments that need to be made.

00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:55.230
We the polluters have to adjust down,

00:05:55.230 --> 00:05:57.350
and then we have a proper
level to work on this

00:05:57.350 --> 00:05:59.623
but the politics of
that are extremely hard.

00:06:01.110 --> 00:06:01.943
- Thank you.

00:06:01.943 --> 00:06:03.470
And when you mentioned
we're all living beyond

00:06:03.470 --> 00:06:05.270
our sustainable environmental means,

00:06:05.270 --> 00:06:07.560
I know your book outlined
some steps that can be taken

00:06:07.560 --> 00:06:10.020
which we'll get into in our conversation.

00:06:10.020 --> 00:06:13.170
But I'd love to go ahead
and bring, let's see.

00:06:13.170 --> 00:06:15.250
We'll begin with Sunil,
if that's all right.

00:06:15.250 --> 00:06:16.280
We'll bring Sunil into this.

00:06:16.280 --> 00:06:19.680
So again, I mentioned that
you and Rohini and Rory

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:21.820
have been part of conversations

00:06:21.820 --> 00:06:22.780
over the course of this series

00:06:22.780 --> 00:06:25.080
where the topic of green
growth has come up.

00:06:25.080 --> 00:06:27.700
And I wonder if you can expand on

00:06:28.670 --> 00:06:30.850
based on maybe historical
perspective as well

00:06:30.850 --> 00:06:33.070
which I know you bring to this,

00:06:33.070 --> 00:06:35.330
what do we do now that
we're at this crossroads?

00:06:35.330 --> 00:06:36.190
Who needs to step up?

00:06:36.190 --> 00:06:37.990
What hard decisions need to be made?

00:06:39.270 --> 00:06:41.440
- In some ways, Catherine,
I think the hardest decision

00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:42.980
is the way you put it just now.

00:06:42.980 --> 00:06:44.483
You said, we all agree.

00:06:45.820 --> 00:06:46.980
We all agree,

00:06:46.980 --> 00:06:50.350
but I suspect a lot of
the world does not agree.

00:06:50.350 --> 00:06:53.130
And I think that the hardest task

00:06:53.130 --> 00:06:55.460
is in fact a profoundly political task.

00:06:55.460 --> 00:06:57.780
And that is the task for
those of us who believe

00:06:57.780 --> 00:07:00.830
that a new way of thinking about growth

00:07:00.830 --> 00:07:02.350
and the relationship between the economy

00:07:02.350 --> 00:07:03.330
and environment is necessarily.

00:07:03.330 --> 00:07:05.740
The task is one of persuasion really,

00:07:05.740 --> 00:07:08.832
because I think there's a big disconnect

00:07:08.832 --> 00:07:10.750
between the kinds of conversations

00:07:10.750 --> 00:07:12.510
that we've been having on this dialogue

00:07:12.510 --> 00:07:15.520
with a wonderful audience engaging with us

00:07:15.520 --> 00:07:18.813
and the conversations that are
being had around the world,

00:07:18.813 --> 00:07:20.870
not just in the developed countries

00:07:20.870 --> 00:07:25.640
but also in developing countries
about who should sacrifice,

00:07:25.640 --> 00:07:27.340
about what priorities

00:07:27.340 --> 00:07:29.723
are particularly in
light of this pandemic.

00:07:31.430 --> 00:07:32.660
- I think you're absolutely right.

00:07:32.660 --> 00:07:35.700
And so if that is a barrier persuasion

00:07:35.700 --> 00:07:37.240
which I think it is,

00:07:37.240 --> 00:07:38.390
hopefully in our conversation,

00:07:38.390 --> 00:07:42.370
we can get to not only hard
decisions that need to be made

00:07:42.370 --> 00:07:46.280
but hard steps that need to
be taken including persuasion

00:07:46.280 --> 00:07:49.490
and consensus before we
can actually make progress.

00:07:49.490 --> 00:07:51.670
So hopefully we can get into that.

00:07:51.670 --> 00:07:53.050
Rohini, can I bring you into this?

00:07:53.050 --> 00:07:58.050
What's your take on this topic
and Net-Zero After COVID-19.

00:07:58.130 --> 00:07:59.880
How do we get there?

00:07:59.880 --> 00:08:01.220
- Thanks, Catherine.

00:08:01.220 --> 00:08:03.350
So I think I'd probably build off both

00:08:03.350 --> 00:08:06.380
what Dieter and Sunil were talking about.

00:08:06.380 --> 00:08:09.670
And can really remind us
that in some ways before,

00:08:09.670 --> 00:08:12.370
there was economics that
was political economy.

00:08:12.370 --> 00:08:16.200
And that really, I think is
what needs to be sent to stage

00:08:16.200 --> 00:08:20.160
as we think about issues
to do with climate change

00:08:20.160 --> 00:08:23.010
and how we are going to
get rich and poor countries

00:08:23.010 --> 00:08:26.170
to possibly choose different
distributional burdens.

00:08:26.170 --> 00:08:29.120
Sunil talked about persuasion.

00:08:29.120 --> 00:08:33.530
I would add to it the idea of
how do we create incentives.

00:08:33.530 --> 00:08:35.380
So how do we actually have policies

00:08:35.380 --> 00:08:37.670
that incentivize rich countries

00:08:37.670 --> 00:08:41.410
to do what is arguably good for the world

00:08:41.410 --> 00:08:43.580
but perhaps may not be good for them

00:08:43.580 --> 00:08:44.890
in some very narrow sense,

00:08:44.890 --> 00:08:46.550
something I know you're
going to come back to

00:08:46.550 --> 00:08:49.570
that we've already seen with COVID-19

00:08:49.570 --> 00:08:52.093
and a lot of discussion
of vaccine nationalism.

00:08:53.190 --> 00:08:56.770
The other issue I'd bring
up is that implementation.

00:08:56.770 --> 00:08:59.760
I think a lot of discussions
of climate change policies

00:08:59.760 --> 00:09:01.640
tend to be quite legal right now.

00:09:01.640 --> 00:09:06.440
So a lot of discussion of having
different countries decide

00:09:07.325 --> 00:09:09.540
what policies to put in place.

00:09:09.540 --> 00:09:12.370
But when we look at I think the failure

00:09:12.370 --> 00:09:14.730
of several of these
policies, for instance,

00:09:14.730 --> 00:09:16.450
those are the Kyoto Protocol.

00:09:16.450 --> 00:09:19.210
A lot of them have to do
again when we can institutions

00:09:19.210 --> 00:09:21.700
then make it hard to
implement these policies.

00:09:21.700 --> 00:09:24.480
And again, looking at COVID-19
and looking for instance

00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:27.440
at the vaccine rollout in
India and how slow it is,

00:09:27.440 --> 00:09:28.740
I think it's important to remember

00:09:28.740 --> 00:09:31.340
that a lot of the issues are going to be

00:09:31.340 --> 00:09:33.510
how do we have fair technology transfers?

00:09:33.510 --> 00:09:35.857
How do we think about IP issues?

00:09:35.857 --> 00:09:38.220
But a lot are going to also be about

00:09:40.101 --> 00:09:43.590
how can the global community
helps trend and institutions

00:09:43.590 --> 00:09:45.567
and how can citizens domestic countries

00:09:45.567 --> 00:09:46.830
ask for the institutions

00:09:46.830 --> 00:09:48.830
that will give them the voice they need.

00:09:50.620 --> 00:09:52.370
- And yeah, I know you can expand on this

00:09:52.370 --> 00:09:53.240
in our conversation

00:09:53.240 --> 00:09:56.810
but I think if we look
at COVID-19 as a test

00:09:56.810 --> 00:10:01.810
for how developed countries can
support developing countries

00:10:02.280 --> 00:10:04.193
going through a shared challenge,

00:10:05.300 --> 00:10:07.660
there are concerning
implications for our ability

00:10:07.660 --> 00:10:09.700
to respond to climate change

00:10:09.700 --> 00:10:13.140
given the challenges
we've seen with COVID-19

00:10:13.140 --> 00:10:15.340
and global solidarity
in responding to that.

00:10:16.270 --> 00:10:17.750
Rory, I'd love to bring you into this.

00:10:17.750 --> 00:10:20.331
I know that your views on these issues

00:10:20.331 --> 00:10:24.610
include your own experience
as working in policy,

00:10:24.610 --> 00:10:25.640
working in development,

00:10:25.640 --> 00:10:29.450
trying to work with countries
facing these hard decisions.

00:10:29.450 --> 00:10:32.530
So what are some of the challenges ahead

00:10:32.530 --> 00:10:34.530
and who needs to do what?

00:10:34.530 --> 00:10:36.250
- Well, I'm really gonna come in on this

00:10:36.250 --> 00:10:37.550
from the point of view of somebody

00:10:37.550 --> 00:10:40.870
who was recently a
politician and a minister.

00:10:40.870 --> 00:10:44.390
And I saw three different types of issue.

00:10:44.390 --> 00:10:46.890
One of them is domestic to Britain.

00:10:46.890 --> 00:10:49.203
So when I was running
to be mayor of London,

00:10:50.680 --> 00:10:52.860
all the polling suggested that

00:10:53.730 --> 00:10:55.940
the overwhelming majority
of the population

00:10:55.940 --> 00:11:00.410
was not prepared to spend
15 pounds more a year

00:11:00.410 --> 00:11:02.310
in order to pursue environmental policies.

00:11:02.310 --> 00:11:04.740
So it's extremely important to understand

00:11:04.740 --> 00:11:06.800
that domestic politicians running

00:11:06.800 --> 00:11:10.760
are being told by that polling
experts and strategists

00:11:10.760 --> 00:11:12.653
that this is not a way to get votes.

00:11:13.490 --> 00:11:15.650
And if you think about the
things that we wanted to do

00:11:15.650 --> 00:11:18.710
even at a tiny scale of London,
for example, on air quality

00:11:18.710 --> 00:11:23.620
extend the clean air zone out
to the outskirts of London

00:11:23.620 --> 00:11:28.500
which was going to pose a
cost of 65 pounds a week

00:11:28.500 --> 00:11:32.860
on people with older vehicles
from low income families,

00:11:32.860 --> 00:11:37.280
you could see why very,
very quickly in the election

00:11:37.280 --> 00:11:39.430
people were reluctant to go there.

00:11:39.430 --> 00:11:40.800
So that's first thing.

00:11:40.800 --> 00:11:43.670
Second thing is the way in
which countries like Britain

00:11:43.670 --> 00:11:44.807
deal with other countries.

00:11:44.807 --> 00:11:47.340
In other words, the
bilateral relationship.

00:11:47.340 --> 00:11:50.650
So I was very struck and defeated
by the enormous reluctance

00:11:50.650 --> 00:11:53.420
from British development professionals

00:11:53.420 --> 00:11:57.253
to really emphasize climate.

00:11:58.290 --> 00:12:01.860
And the reason for that is
that their development models

00:12:01.860 --> 00:12:05.120
very much dependent
particularly on energy.

00:12:05.120 --> 00:12:07.870
So a lots of the programs
that they were most proud of

00:12:07.870 --> 00:12:09.800
which they thought would
get the greatest results

00:12:09.800 --> 00:12:12.316
in terms of getting people out of poverty

00:12:12.316 --> 00:12:17.316
involved investing in coal
fired, heavy oil fired plants.

00:12:17.930 --> 00:12:19.590
And when push came to shove,

00:12:19.590 --> 00:12:21.470
when I started to block those projects,

00:12:21.470 --> 00:12:25.350
so I started block money
going to a Shell Oil project

00:12:25.350 --> 00:12:26.530
in Senegal, for example,

00:12:26.530 --> 00:12:29.560
or I tried to stop the Commonwealth
Development Corporation

00:12:29.560 --> 00:12:33.280
spending its money on large
fossil fuel burning projects

00:12:33.280 --> 00:12:37.360
in Africa, there was huge
pushback in almost reluctance.

00:12:37.360 --> 00:12:41.310
Even though the development
community acknowledges that

00:12:41.310 --> 00:12:42.640
a lot of the impacts of climate change

00:12:42.640 --> 00:12:44.590
will be felt very, very drastically

00:12:44.590 --> 00:12:46.340
by some of the poorest people in the world

00:12:46.340 --> 00:12:48.270
in terms of flooding, in
terms of solar iteration,

00:12:48.270 --> 00:12:49.603
in terms of heat, right?

00:12:50.700 --> 00:12:53.740
And the argument made by the
most senior person to fit

00:12:53.740 --> 00:12:56.510
is that by dealing with climate change,

00:12:56.510 --> 00:12:59.000
we were punishing today's poor

00:12:59.000 --> 00:13:00.600
for the sake of tomorrow's poor.

00:13:01.490 --> 00:13:04.180
And then the final point
which we can turn back to

00:13:04.180 --> 00:13:08.170
which I think Rohini, Sunil
and Dieter have all touched on

00:13:09.190 --> 00:13:11.070
is the way that this is actually perceived

00:13:11.070 --> 00:13:12.120
from other countries,

00:13:13.038 --> 00:13:17.760
and how much India may be reluctant

00:13:17.760 --> 00:13:20.070
to accept these type of moves.

00:13:20.070 --> 00:13:22.290
But to take it even more
extreme, think about Afghanistan.

00:13:22.290 --> 00:13:24.550
When I walked across Afghanistan

00:13:24.550 --> 00:13:26.150
in the winter of 2001, 2002,

00:13:26.150 --> 00:13:29.850
there was literally no electricity
between Herat and Kabul,

00:13:29.850 --> 00:13:30.697
none at all.

00:13:31.750 --> 00:13:33.400
And as Afghanistan tries to develop,

00:13:33.400 --> 00:13:35.380
obviously they're trying to
generate some electricity

00:13:35.380 --> 00:13:37.743
but this is a country
of 30 million people.

00:13:39.070 --> 00:13:42.320
If Afghanistan began to go on the path

00:13:42.320 --> 00:13:44.880
of trying to generate
the amount of electricity

00:13:44.880 --> 00:13:46.867
that a European country
of 30 million people got

00:13:46.867 --> 00:13:50.740
and that that was repeated
cause you cannot imagine

00:13:50.740 --> 00:13:52.350
the scale of the problem
we're creating for the world.

00:13:52.350 --> 00:13:55.147
And yet, can you imagine
trying to say to Afghans,

00:13:55.147 --> 00:13:58.700
no, no, no, I'm sorry,
this isn't gonna happen.

00:13:58.700 --> 00:14:00.740
Okay, so on those three
points, I'm gonna stop

00:14:00.740 --> 00:14:03.210
'cause everybody else
was much shorter than me.

00:14:03.210 --> 00:14:05.060
- Well, I do have one
follow-up question for you

00:14:05.060 --> 00:14:07.610
which is when you're facing
that kind of pushback

00:14:07.610 --> 00:14:09.380
and that statement as you said,

00:14:09.380 --> 00:14:10.670
by dealing with climate change,

00:14:10.670 --> 00:14:13.453
we're punishing today's
poor for tomorrow's poor.

00:14:17.620 --> 00:14:19.320
It's hard to make change, right?

00:14:19.320 --> 00:14:21.660
Even just institutionally,
no matter the change,

00:14:21.660 --> 00:14:22.930
it's hard to make change.

00:14:22.930 --> 00:14:26.190
And you mentioned that
these energy projects

00:14:26.190 --> 00:14:29.460
which within defeat, there
were a lot of champions

00:14:29.460 --> 00:14:30.630
for these energy projects

00:14:30.630 --> 00:14:32.230
in terms of their development outcomes,

00:14:32.230 --> 00:14:35.363
the environmental impacts were negative.

00:14:36.910 --> 00:14:38.370
If you're making that kind of argument

00:14:38.370 --> 00:14:39.470
and seeing that kind of pushback,

00:14:39.470 --> 00:14:40.490
it seems that it would be helpful

00:14:40.490 --> 00:14:42.240
to point to examples of what could work.

00:14:42.240 --> 00:14:45.010
And can you give some
examples what could work?

00:14:45.010 --> 00:14:46.900
What could be a win-win for development

00:14:46.900 --> 00:14:48.730
and for the environment?

00:14:48.730 --> 00:14:52.080
- I mean, it is true that you can do

00:14:52.080 --> 00:14:55.710
some extraordinary renewable
energy projects now

00:14:55.710 --> 00:14:58.480
much more cheaply than you
were able to a few years ago.

00:14:58.480 --> 00:15:03.190
So I went to see this
extraordinary wind project

00:15:03.190 --> 00:15:04.880
in Lake Turkana in Kenya

00:15:04.880 --> 00:15:07.230
and I went to see some
extraordinary solar projects

00:15:07.230 --> 00:15:08.570
in Jordan.

00:15:08.570 --> 00:15:10.340
And they are generating
electricity in Jordan

00:15:10.340 --> 00:15:13.000
from solar panels at
an incredibly low rate.

00:15:13.000 --> 00:15:15.835
But I just want to dig
into that a little bit

00:15:15.835 --> 00:15:19.670
because of course, one
forgets how politics matters

00:15:19.670 --> 00:15:21.620
even with something like a solar panel.

00:15:22.460 --> 00:15:24.440
So although they were
generating electricity

00:15:24.440 --> 00:15:25.890
from these set of panels much more cheaply

00:15:25.890 --> 00:15:27.910
than they were generating
extras from anything else,

00:15:27.910 --> 00:15:29.250
they weren't building anymore.

00:15:29.250 --> 00:15:32.480
And the reason they weren't
building anymore was politics.

00:15:32.480 --> 00:15:34.685
The Arab Spring had led to them

00:15:34.685 --> 00:15:37.620
losing their fuel supply from Egypt.

00:15:37.620 --> 00:15:41.330
They then sign the series of
contracts for 20 or 30 years

00:15:42.400 --> 00:15:45.690
with big international oil companies

00:15:45.690 --> 00:15:49.210
to produce carbon produced
the energy into Jordan

00:15:49.210 --> 00:15:50.500
and they couldn't get
out of those contracts.

00:15:50.500 --> 00:15:53.640
They had to pay the money to
them every year, regardless.

00:15:53.640 --> 00:15:57.110
But there was no point of
building more solar panels

00:15:57.110 --> 00:15:59.920
for domestic consumption
and they couldn't export

00:15:59.920 --> 00:16:01.450
because all their export lines run

00:16:01.450 --> 00:16:05.410
through Syria to Turkey and
Iraq which had a huge demand

00:16:05.410 --> 00:16:07.603
for the type of power
they were able to produce.

00:16:08.720 --> 00:16:10.260
I just wanted to bring that to bare

00:16:10.260 --> 00:16:13.010
because it's very tempting for
students and others to think,

00:16:13.010 --> 00:16:15.375
okay, well, why don't we
just can't give Michele

00:16:15.375 --> 00:16:16.208
the solar of panels,

00:16:16.208 --> 00:16:19.180
and Mali with set of panels,
Jordan with set of panels.

00:16:19.180 --> 00:16:21.250
But when you actually
get into these problems

00:16:21.250 --> 00:16:23.530
the way the contracts work
and the way that the politics

00:16:23.530 --> 00:16:25.070
and conflicts in neighboring states work,

00:16:25.070 --> 00:16:26.560
these things become much more difficult.

00:16:26.560 --> 00:16:28.170
I'm sorry, I'll try and
be a bit more optimistic

00:16:28.170 --> 00:16:29.680
in my next time.

00:16:29.680 --> 00:16:30.920
- No, that was great, Rory.

00:16:30.920 --> 00:16:33.753
And I want to bring Rohini in
because India came up earlier.

00:16:33.753 --> 00:16:37.670
And just in terms of zooming
in on what this looks like

00:16:37.670 --> 00:16:40.560
and how politics are
involved as Rory mentioned,

00:16:40.560 --> 00:16:43.100
Rohini, can you provide
us some perspective

00:16:43.100 --> 00:16:43.933
looking at India.

00:16:43.933 --> 00:16:47.173
- Well, I see a train passed
from outside my house.

00:16:47.173 --> 00:16:49.930
- I'm timing with a train
passing through, okay.

00:16:49.930 --> 00:16:50.763
- But let me jump.

00:16:50.763 --> 00:16:54.600
Actually, I wanted to just
follow up on Rory's point

00:16:54.600 --> 00:16:58.010
on solar pricing and
actually talk about some work

00:16:58.010 --> 00:17:00.260
that one of our colleagues here at Yale,

00:17:00.260 --> 00:17:02.440
Nicholas Ryan has been doing right now

00:17:04.806 --> 00:17:08.820
on solar pricing and growth
of solar energy in India.

00:17:08.820 --> 00:17:10.777
And I think the point he's made is that

00:17:10.777 --> 00:17:13.270
green energy suppliers now increasingly

00:17:13.270 --> 00:17:16.860
have as reset low what we
would call variable cost.

00:17:16.860 --> 00:17:19.600
So they still have a large fixed cost.

00:17:19.600 --> 00:17:21.930
This is partly the case
also that as you're thinking

00:17:21.930 --> 00:17:23.620
about alternative forms of energy,

00:17:23.620 --> 00:17:24.550
we often have to think about

00:17:24.550 --> 00:17:27.300
how they're going to fit into the grid.

00:17:27.300 --> 00:17:29.300
So what this has meant
in the case of India

00:17:29.300 --> 00:17:30.910
which is a federal country,

00:17:30.910 --> 00:17:35.910
that if you're a producer of
say green energy supplier,

00:17:36.290 --> 00:17:38.690
you have to contract
with the state government

00:17:38.690 --> 00:17:41.050
in order to say, I will give
you so much solar energy

00:17:41.050 --> 00:17:42.830
that'll go into your grid.

00:17:42.830 --> 00:17:44.950
But what this means is that

00:17:44.950 --> 00:17:46.960
most electricity distribution companies

00:17:46.960 --> 00:17:49.780
at a state level in India
are terribly bankrupt

00:17:49.780 --> 00:17:53.810
for a combination of political
economy and other reasons.

00:17:53.810 --> 00:17:55.640
And so there's a huge concern

00:17:55.640 --> 00:17:57.960
of basically renegotiation and default

00:17:57.960 --> 00:18:00.100
by the state electricity companies

00:18:00.100 --> 00:18:03.670
which will very often not be
taken to court the same way.

00:18:03.670 --> 00:18:05.690
And so this has led to in general

00:18:05.690 --> 00:18:10.690
quite a lot of counterparty
risk for green energy suppliers

00:18:10.732 --> 00:18:13.060
when they're trying to go
to the state government.

00:18:13.060 --> 00:18:15.070
So they're very willing in Nick's work

00:18:15.070 --> 00:18:16.790
right now to sell to
the central government

00:18:16.790 --> 00:18:18.650
which seems to be doing better here,

00:18:18.650 --> 00:18:21.510
but very unwilling, very
often to go to those states

00:18:21.510 --> 00:18:23.210
that are actually the poorest states

00:18:23.210 --> 00:18:25.960
that are just beginning
to put in place grids

00:18:25.960 --> 00:18:28.180
when you really want from the start

00:18:28.180 --> 00:18:30.800
a transition to green energy.

00:18:30.800 --> 00:18:32.140
But those are exactly the states

00:18:32.140 --> 00:18:33.440
where the suppliers don't want to go

00:18:33.440 --> 00:18:34.960
and because they're the most concerned

00:18:34.960 --> 00:18:37.720
that they will not be
able to recover that cost.

00:18:37.720 --> 00:18:39.590
So I think that's a good example
of saying that very often

00:18:39.590 --> 00:18:40.740
we look at technology and we say,

00:18:40.740 --> 00:18:42.120
wow, look at the price of solar.

00:18:42.120 --> 00:18:43.860
It's falling so fast.

00:18:43.860 --> 00:18:45.580
And we have to actually think about

00:18:45.580 --> 00:18:48.110
what actually is a good to take

00:18:48.110 --> 00:18:50.450
for the suppliers to provide it.

00:18:50.450 --> 00:18:52.040
As Rory said, sometimes international

00:18:52.040 --> 00:18:55.771
but even within a fast-growing
country like India,

00:18:55.771 --> 00:18:59.260
it can be institutional
barriers or just risks

00:18:59.260 --> 00:19:01.680
that means people won't tend to.

00:19:01.680 --> 00:19:03.957
- Absolutely, I write
a lot about technology

00:19:03.957 --> 00:19:05.010
and global development.

00:19:05.010 --> 00:19:06.630
And there is a lot of promise

00:19:06.630 --> 00:19:08.070
when it comes to technology in this space,

00:19:08.070 --> 00:19:10.410
but that what will it take question?

00:19:10.410 --> 00:19:12.330
Many barriers stand in the way.

00:19:12.330 --> 00:19:15.500
And speaking of that, I
want to return to Dieter now

00:19:15.500 --> 00:19:17.930
and hear a little bit more about your book

00:19:17.930 --> 00:19:20.500
and what you lay out in the book.

00:19:20.500 --> 00:19:23.510
And I know you talk about what citizens

00:19:23.510 --> 00:19:25.600
and governments can do to cut carbon.

00:19:25.600 --> 00:19:28.660
And we've heard a little bit from Rory

00:19:28.660 --> 00:19:31.720
about some of the challenges
working in government,

00:19:31.720 --> 00:19:33.580
Rohini expanded on that.

00:19:33.580 --> 00:19:36.520
I'd love to narrow the focus
to citizens for a moment

00:19:36.520 --> 00:19:37.770
because earlier you were talking

00:19:37.770 --> 00:19:41.170
about this as a shared responsibility.

00:19:41.170 --> 00:19:43.760
And for citizens in low
and middle income countries

00:19:43.760 --> 00:19:45.370
in particular, I'm curious

00:19:46.610 --> 00:19:49.410
kind of building on Rory's point earlier,

00:19:49.410 --> 00:19:51.373
what should they be asked to give up?

00:19:54.810 --> 00:19:56.450
Again, many challenges here

00:19:56.450 --> 00:20:00.624
in terms of asking people to
do things for the environment

00:20:00.624 --> 00:20:03.320
that might not be in
their own self-interest

00:20:03.320 --> 00:20:04.830
or in the interest of their country

00:20:04.830 --> 00:20:07.260
from a development perspective

00:20:07.260 --> 00:20:09.320
but I'd love for you to
expand on points in your book

00:20:09.320 --> 00:20:11.780
in terms of if we are all to take action,

00:20:11.780 --> 00:20:13.130
what does that action look like

00:20:13.130 --> 00:20:14.460
from the perspective of citizens

00:20:14.460 --> 00:20:16.043
in low and middle income country?

00:20:18.260 --> 00:20:20.820
And you're on mute.
Yeah.

00:20:20.820 --> 00:20:23.563
- Use the same lock in the end often yet.

00:20:26.540 --> 00:20:29.390
It's important if you are
citizens to do things.

00:20:29.390 --> 00:20:31.597
And I deliberately focused on citizens

00:20:31.597 --> 00:20:34.170
and not just consumers

00:20:34.170 --> 00:20:36.610
and I'll explain that point in a moment.

00:20:36.610 --> 00:20:38.890
You have to make sure
that what they're doing

00:20:38.890 --> 00:20:41.140
is gonna make some difference

00:20:41.140 --> 00:20:43.360
and it's actually going
to address the problem.

00:20:43.360 --> 00:20:45.503
So you have to start back at the beginning

00:20:45.503 --> 00:20:50.503
with characterizing what the
climate problem is, where it is

00:20:50.927 --> 00:20:54.400
and what would be necessary
to crack this problem.

00:20:54.400 --> 00:20:56.020
So the first thing to say is

00:20:56.020 --> 00:20:58.880
almost all the discussion
is about emissions.

00:20:58.880 --> 00:21:00.000
But then the number that matters

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:02.530
is the concentration
of carbon in the attic,

00:21:02.530 --> 00:21:05.410
the stock and the other greenhouse gases.

00:21:05.410 --> 00:21:09.280
It's sequestration and emissions,

00:21:09.280 --> 00:21:12.150
and its emissions net of sequestration

00:21:12.150 --> 00:21:14.220
which adds to that concentration.

00:21:14.220 --> 00:21:16.560
So it's just as important to talk about

00:21:16.560 --> 00:21:18.190
what's happening to the Amazon

00:21:18.190 --> 00:21:21.290
and how you protect the
natural assets around the world

00:21:21.290 --> 00:21:24.770
as it is to talk about what
comes out of a coal postage.

00:21:24.770 --> 00:21:27.080
It's not that the coal
postage is important,

00:21:27.080 --> 00:21:29.000
it's just that we shouldn't be

00:21:29.000 --> 00:21:31.320
exclusively focused on emissions

00:21:31.320 --> 00:21:32.740
without thinking about sequestration.

00:21:32.740 --> 00:21:35.960
The things like rainforest
in Brazil, it's both,

00:21:35.960 --> 00:21:38.110
it's admitting a hell of
a lot by burning it down,

00:21:38.110 --> 00:21:40.050
but it should be
sequestrating a lot instead

00:21:40.050 --> 00:21:40.883
and oceans, phytoplanktons and so on.

00:21:43.270 --> 00:21:44.490
So that's the first thing to say.

00:21:44.490 --> 00:21:48.310
The second thing to say is
anyone who thinks one more heave

00:21:48.310 --> 00:21:51.760
and that'll I'm sure lead
to a discussion about COP26

00:21:51.760 --> 00:21:55.340
has to explain how a bit of recent history

00:21:56.490 --> 00:21:57.970
doesn't fit that model.

00:21:57.970 --> 00:22:01.400
So every single year since 1990,

00:22:01.400 --> 00:22:03.030
we've added two parts per million

00:22:03.030 --> 00:22:05.770
to the concentration of
carbon in the atmosphere.

00:22:05.770 --> 00:22:10.000
There isn't a blip for the
financial crisis in '07, '08,

00:22:10.000 --> 00:22:13.760
and there was no blip last
year during the lockdowns.

00:22:13.760 --> 00:22:17.410
And that should really give
people pause for thought.

00:22:17.410 --> 00:22:20.562
If you do all of that to
the developed economies

00:22:20.562 --> 00:22:24.070
and still you are two parts
per million to the atmosphere,

00:22:24.070 --> 00:22:26.330
we really have to look at
this problem very hard.

00:22:26.330 --> 00:22:29.800
I mean, I'm happy for
everyone to have meet up

00:22:29.800 --> 00:22:33.030
for 17,000 people to rock up in Glasgow.

00:22:33.030 --> 00:22:34.500
I'm very pleased that politicians

00:22:34.500 --> 00:22:36.330
are talking about climate change.

00:22:36.330 --> 00:22:38.560
But anyone thinks that Paris
really cracked the problem

00:22:38.560 --> 00:22:40.320
and this is the icing on the cake.

00:22:40.320 --> 00:22:42.540
They're not in the same universe as me.

00:22:42.540 --> 00:22:44.350
Now, the next thing that's very important

00:22:44.350 --> 00:22:47.350
and this is back to which citizens, okay.

00:22:47.350 --> 00:22:49.900
If you look at current climate change,

00:22:49.900 --> 00:22:54.330
nearly 30% of emissions
are from China, okay.

00:22:54.330 --> 00:22:58.930
China has more emissions than
Europe and the United States

00:22:58.930 --> 00:23:02.220
put together now in the
share of total emissions.

00:23:02.220 --> 00:23:04.680
These are staggering numbers, okay?

00:23:04.680 --> 00:23:08.090
And what's more, it's building
more new coal power stations

00:23:08.090 --> 00:23:10.300
than all the coal power stations closing

00:23:10.300 --> 00:23:12.240
in Europe and in America.

00:23:12.240 --> 00:23:15.780
Good to that is it's important,
but it's in proportion.

00:23:15.780 --> 00:23:17.780
And that the current growth rate of China

00:23:17.780 --> 00:23:22.780
let's say five to 6% by
235, there'll be two Chinas.

00:23:23.900 --> 00:23:27.390
So to get a real grip of the
magnitude of the problem,

00:23:27.390 --> 00:23:30.350
think about a world in
which you just go off shore

00:23:30.350 --> 00:23:31.510
from the East coast of China

00:23:31.510 --> 00:23:32.357
and there's another one

00:23:32.357 --> 00:23:35.370
and it looks very much
like the existing one.

00:23:35.370 --> 00:23:38.010
And that will probably
be true of India too

00:23:38.010 --> 00:23:42.090
as India's growth rate goes
back up to five six and beyond.

00:23:42.090 --> 00:23:43.860
And you can just do the arithmetic.

00:23:43.860 --> 00:23:45.360
So these are stunning numbers.

00:23:46.490 --> 00:23:48.770
So if you go into that context and say,

00:23:48.770 --> 00:23:51.890
so what does it take
to crack that problem?

00:23:51.890 --> 00:23:54.490
Well, if you just look at emissions,

00:23:54.490 --> 00:23:57.410
you think it's the Chinese,
it's all their fault.

00:23:57.410 --> 00:24:00.290
I've heard politicians
say those things privately

00:24:00.290 --> 00:24:01.660
going forward, okay.

00:24:01.660 --> 00:24:03.120
So there are two things to say about it.

00:24:03.120 --> 00:24:06.893
One is that a lot of those
emissions are for you and me.

00:24:07.780 --> 00:24:09.930
So the Chinese model is less so now

00:24:09.930 --> 00:24:12.740
but it was built on an
export orientated model

00:24:12.740 --> 00:24:15.790
as an Indian model may be going forward

00:24:15.790 --> 00:24:18.590
following on from Korea,
Japan, and Germany

00:24:18.590 --> 00:24:22.790
in the periods after the
post second world war period.

00:24:22.790 --> 00:24:25.393
So you have to make sure

00:24:25.393 --> 00:24:28.090
that the people who are
actually causing those emissions

00:24:28.090 --> 00:24:30.067
to be made actually are the people

00:24:30.067 --> 00:24:32.558
who are paying for those emissions.

00:24:32.558 --> 00:24:36.450
The piece I'd add on top of that is that

00:24:36.450 --> 00:24:38.730
if you think climate
change is gonna get solved

00:24:38.730 --> 00:24:40.910
between now and 2050 or beyond,

00:24:40.910 --> 00:24:44.499
then it's almost not all
but largely going to be

00:24:44.499 --> 00:24:47.780
in terms of additional
contribution to climate change

00:24:47.780 --> 00:24:50.550
gonna be coming from
China, India and Africa,

00:24:50.550 --> 00:24:53.850
and Brazil, Philippines, Indonesia.

00:24:53.850 --> 00:24:56.890
These are the rapidly
growing areas in the world.

00:24:56.890 --> 00:25:01.430
And if they do rapidly grow
with high levels of emission,

00:25:01.430 --> 00:25:03.890
whatever the justice of that,

00:25:03.890 --> 00:25:06.670
we will face that very hot climate change.

00:25:06.670 --> 00:25:11.040
So if we are really interested
in making these changes,

00:25:11.040 --> 00:25:13.680
we the citizens of the countries

00:25:13.680 --> 00:25:16.150
with much higher consumptive per head

00:25:16.150 --> 00:25:18.290
have to make sure we pay for the emissions

00:25:18.290 --> 00:25:19.920
in those countries.

00:25:19.920 --> 00:25:22.740
And now what do their citizens do?

00:25:22.740 --> 00:25:24.313
Well, I'd put it the other way around.

00:25:24.313 --> 00:25:26.950
What are their citizens entitled to?

00:25:26.950 --> 00:25:31.560
So I say citizens because
in my environmental approach

00:25:31.560 --> 00:25:35.880
what I want to do is to
make sure that all citizens

00:25:35.880 --> 00:25:39.370
have the basic capabilities
to function society.

00:25:39.370 --> 00:25:41.770
You can see that I did my
thesis under much ascend

00:25:41.770 --> 00:25:44.020
to see where this argument comes from.

00:25:44.020 --> 00:25:48.030
So it's a positive entitlement
to assets not utility,

00:25:48.030 --> 00:25:50.220
not the economist normal currency,

00:25:50.220 --> 00:25:52.260
it's making sure they have the wherewithal

00:25:52.260 --> 00:25:54.150
within which to live their lives.

00:25:54.150 --> 00:25:56.710
The most important wherewithal to do that

00:25:56.710 --> 00:25:59.830
is to have the natural capital assets,

00:25:59.830 --> 00:26:03.150
environmental assets to
make their lives tolerable.

00:26:03.150 --> 00:26:04.850
The only way that's gonna happen

00:26:04.850 --> 00:26:07.140
as the Indian Energy Minister

00:26:07.140 --> 00:26:09.520
made clear a couple of weeks ago

00:26:09.520 --> 00:26:13.030
is that there has to be
substantial transfers

00:26:13.030 --> 00:26:14.780
to make that possible.

00:26:14.780 --> 00:26:17.470
And I think the most
frightening thing about Paris

00:26:17.470 --> 00:26:20.270
and they need the
discussions about Glasgow

00:26:20.270 --> 00:26:21.740
is that people are talking about,

00:26:21.740 --> 00:26:25.580
well, couldn't we go from
100 billion to 200 billion

00:26:25.580 --> 00:26:29.508
to address the transfers
that might be required.

00:26:29.508 --> 00:26:34.422
We in Britain spent 300
billion just on COVID, right.

00:26:34.422 --> 00:26:36.960
This has got an order
of magnitude different.

00:26:36.960 --> 00:26:39.900
And the difference between
the COVID it episode

00:26:39.900 --> 00:26:43.200
and the climate change
one is the COVID one

00:26:43.200 --> 00:26:47.650
enables an element of
national wool building

00:26:47.650 --> 00:26:52.260
and nationalistic protection
which we've seen that lives.

00:26:52.260 --> 00:26:54.180
There is no wall you can build,

00:26:54.180 --> 00:26:56.540
not even one that Mr. Trump could imagine

00:26:56.540 --> 00:26:58.590
which will keep a ton of carbon emitted

00:26:58.590 --> 00:27:01.530
anywhere out of the
world into our climate.

00:27:01.530 --> 00:27:03.807
So I think we have to
think about global citizens

00:27:03.807 --> 00:27:05.540
and their entitlements,

00:27:05.540 --> 00:27:09.090
we have to think about
isn't assets not flows

00:27:09.090 --> 00:27:10.140
and we have to think about it

00:27:10.140 --> 00:27:13.140
in the capabilities that go with that.

00:27:13.140 --> 00:27:14.560
Not just trying to make them happy

00:27:14.560 --> 00:27:16.890
and think it's some of you tyrant exercise

00:27:16.890 --> 00:27:18.693
in maximizing economic welfare.

00:27:20.020 --> 00:27:22.100
- Thank you, and I'm glad
you brought up this point

00:27:22.100 --> 00:27:24.580
around natural capital which
I hope we can return to

00:27:24.580 --> 00:27:27.770
because I think too often,
it's presented as a trade off

00:27:27.770 --> 00:27:32.280
in terms of protecting
the planet at a cost

00:27:32.280 --> 00:27:34.930
to your country's development path.

00:27:34.930 --> 00:27:36.940
But when you bring in natural capital

00:27:36.940 --> 00:27:39.050
as a lens through which to look at this,

00:27:39.050 --> 00:27:42.297
you can identify more of
these win-win opportunities.

00:27:42.297 --> 00:27:43.850
- A very quick come back on that.

00:27:43.850 --> 00:27:45.433
So a lot of people who
think there's a trade off

00:27:45.433 --> 00:27:48.300
between the environment
and economic growth,

00:27:48.300 --> 00:27:51.787
between that capital
and GDP tomorrow, okay.

00:27:51.787 --> 00:27:53.200
And at the same breadth,

00:27:53.200 --> 00:27:55.060
they'll accept that what
we're currently doing

00:27:55.060 --> 00:27:56.820
is unsustainable.

00:27:56.820 --> 00:27:58.750
So the conclusion that follows

00:27:58.750 --> 00:28:00.370
from something that's unsustainable is

00:28:00.370 --> 00:28:02.770
it will not be sustained.

00:28:02.770 --> 00:28:05.770
And the problem is and the
problem of convincing people

00:28:05.770 --> 00:28:08.930
in Europe and the United States is that

00:28:08.930 --> 00:28:11.180
the people who are really
gonna get the bronc end

00:28:11.180 --> 00:28:14.400
of it not being sustained
is actually not us.

00:28:14.400 --> 00:28:17.220
We've got a lot of technologies,
we've got air conditioning,

00:28:17.220 --> 00:28:19.010
we've got all sorts of things.

00:28:19.010 --> 00:28:21.220
It's the poor that are
gonna get the consequences.

00:28:21.220 --> 00:28:23.320
That's what makes the politics nasty

00:28:23.320 --> 00:28:26.000
because it says, actually, you know what?

00:28:26.000 --> 00:28:29.385
If we don't do what's necessary
to address climate change,

00:28:29.385 --> 00:28:33.240
we get the effects last,
they get them first.

00:28:33.240 --> 00:28:35.190
And that sequencing is quite important.

00:28:37.060 --> 00:28:37.893
- [Catherine] Go ahead, Rory.

00:28:37.893 --> 00:28:38.760
- Come in very quickly.

00:28:38.760 --> 00:28:42.530
I mean, I think Dieter has
produced the very stuck

00:28:42.530 --> 00:28:44.420
and very important thing there.

00:28:44.420 --> 00:28:46.840
Remember it's also true to some extent

00:28:46.840 --> 00:28:51.240
within wealthy countries that
it's often the poorest people

00:28:51.240 --> 00:28:54.393
who are at the center of
this political problem.

00:28:55.330 --> 00:28:58.210
But if you think of relatively
straightforward policies

00:28:58.210 --> 00:29:00.490
that British governments
tried to implement,

00:29:00.490 --> 00:29:03.110
for example, we tried
to increase fuel tax.

00:29:03.110 --> 00:29:04.830
There was an escalator set up

00:29:04.830 --> 00:29:07.200
where fuel tax was meant
to go up every year.

00:29:07.200 --> 00:29:08.353
That was stopped.

00:29:09.500 --> 00:29:11.560
The Labour Party campaign very strongly

00:29:11.560 --> 00:29:13.693
on trying to control energy prices.

00:29:14.680 --> 00:29:19.680
Because the people that
increasingly are the focus

00:29:19.840 --> 00:29:22.120
of political activity,

00:29:22.120 --> 00:29:24.720
could be the Rust Belt
in the United States,

00:29:24.720 --> 00:29:26.450
could be the red wall voters

00:29:26.450 --> 00:29:29.660
in Britain and in North East Britain

00:29:29.660 --> 00:29:32.180
are often people who already feel

00:29:32.180 --> 00:29:34.060
that their incomes have stagnated,

00:29:34.060 --> 00:29:36.410
that there is extraordinary inequality,

00:29:36.410 --> 00:29:39.220
that they are really struggling to get by.

00:29:39.220 --> 00:29:42.610
And that basic things like running car

00:29:42.610 --> 00:29:43.477
or heating their house

00:29:43.477 --> 00:29:46.253
are taking a very large
amount of their income.

00:29:47.600 --> 00:29:50.150
So many of the most
straightforward policies

00:29:50.150 --> 00:29:52.560
that we would want to pursue
will have an impact on them.

00:29:52.560 --> 00:29:54.360
And that's gonna also to relate

00:29:54.360 --> 00:29:56.860
to something at the heart
of Dieter's proposal.

00:29:56.860 --> 00:30:00.610
Dieter essentially is
proposing that there is a tax

00:30:00.610 --> 00:30:02.870
on our consuming of these products, right?

00:30:02.870 --> 00:30:07.440
So the more products that
we consume made in China,

00:30:07.440 --> 00:30:09.113
the more that we will pay.

00:30:10.590 --> 00:30:14.187
But of course the people
who will feel that most

00:30:14.187 --> 00:30:15.900
are the people that are driving

00:30:15.900 --> 00:30:17.390
a lot of the political discontent

00:30:17.390 --> 00:30:19.400
and they're defining a lot of the politics

00:30:19.400 --> 00:30:21.251
in Europe and the United States.

00:30:21.251 --> 00:30:22.084
Thank you.

00:30:23.170 --> 00:30:25.020
- Thank you, and on this point about,

00:30:25.890 --> 00:30:27.410
I think Rory, you teed it up nicely.

00:30:27.410 --> 00:30:30.630
It's not just about rich
versus poor countries,

00:30:30.630 --> 00:30:32.970
it's about poverty and
inequality within countries.

00:30:32.970 --> 00:30:35.010
I know that's something
Rohini can speak to.

00:30:35.010 --> 00:30:36.340
Do you wanna jump in on this?

00:30:36.340 --> 00:30:37.940
- Yeah, I'll just briefly jump in

00:30:37.940 --> 00:30:41.233
and maybe also be a little
bit more provocative.

00:30:42.700 --> 00:30:45.150
I think you can only go so
far if you think of this

00:30:45.150 --> 00:30:47.990
in terms of geography and countries.

00:30:47.990 --> 00:30:51.280
Because in the end, there
are many people in countries

00:30:51.280 --> 00:30:53.120
like India and Brazil and even China

00:30:53.120 --> 00:30:55.630
who are really not consuming very much.

00:30:55.630 --> 00:30:57.850
And it may be that at the country level,

00:30:57.850 --> 00:30:59.550
that's where the costs are,

00:30:59.550 --> 00:31:03.330
but we have to recognize that
consumption of the poorest

00:31:03.330 --> 00:31:05.530
is still something that
needs to rise a lot.

00:31:06.370 --> 00:31:09.460
Recently I saw that was
interesting was a few years ago,

00:31:09.460 --> 00:31:13.500
Bronco Milanovich had
made very well-known,

00:31:13.500 --> 00:31:15.620
there's an elephant curve talking

00:31:15.620 --> 00:31:17.730
about global income inequality.

00:31:17.730 --> 00:31:20.330
I recently saw an Oxfam report that had

00:31:20.330 --> 00:31:22.470
what they call the dinosaur graph

00:31:22.470 --> 00:31:26.840
similarly showing how
emission's varied by income.

00:31:26.840 --> 00:31:29.150
And showed that basically

00:31:29.150 --> 00:31:32.820
if you try to do emission counting

00:31:32.820 --> 00:31:34.940
not to the level of countries,

00:31:34.940 --> 00:31:37.320
but at the level of individuals
you see the same pattern

00:31:37.320 --> 00:31:40.300
that there is the rich who
are really consuming a lot.

00:31:40.300 --> 00:31:41.603
So then what we need to
start thinking hard about

00:31:41.603 --> 00:31:43.540
when we think of this cuts

00:31:43.540 --> 00:31:46.410
is not to have all the
discussion at the country level,

00:31:46.410 --> 00:31:49.030
but move it down to again
what I think is happening

00:31:49.030 --> 00:31:51.840
in the income space
thinking about wealth tax

00:31:51.840 --> 00:31:53.400
or inequality tax.

00:31:53.400 --> 00:31:56.710
And the one final point
just to think about that is

00:31:56.710 --> 00:31:59.350
we need to start thinking about
corporations and companies

00:31:59.350 --> 00:32:01.260
as a component wealth tax.

00:32:01.260 --> 00:32:03.520
So for instance, very often I talked about

00:32:03.520 --> 00:32:06.030
how solar providers are finding it hard

00:32:06.030 --> 00:32:08.880
to enter Indian states.

00:32:08.880 --> 00:32:10.600
Nick actually has an earlier papers

00:32:10.600 --> 00:32:12.710
showing how fossil fuel
companies tend to enter

00:32:12.710 --> 00:32:15.740
these 30 year contracts of its states

00:32:15.740 --> 00:32:17.440
and then renegotiate them.

00:32:17.440 --> 00:32:18.740
So there wouldn't be many cases

00:32:18.740 --> 00:32:21.660
where just as I think in
the income inequality space,

00:32:21.660 --> 00:32:24.010
there's now a very robust discussion

00:32:24.010 --> 00:32:26.340
about the need for wealth tax in the US,

00:32:26.340 --> 00:32:28.470
some kind of corporate taxation in the UK.

00:32:28.470 --> 00:32:31.530
We need to start thinking about
in the environmental space

00:32:31.530 --> 00:32:33.820
at the level of what we think
are the large individuals

00:32:33.820 --> 00:32:35.470
and corporations that are emitting,

00:32:35.470 --> 00:32:37.343
not make it just about countries.

00:32:38.710 --> 00:32:40.600
- So I want to bring Sunil in here

00:32:40.600 --> 00:32:41.820
with a historical perspective.

00:32:41.820 --> 00:32:43.670
India has come up a few times.

00:32:43.670 --> 00:32:46.270
And as I mentioned earlier and
as many of you are following,

00:32:46.270 --> 00:32:50.920
I'm sure as this country faces
the second wave of COVID-19,

00:32:50.920 --> 00:32:52.100
it's a real crisis.

00:32:52.100 --> 00:32:54.700
And Sunil, over the course of this series,

00:32:54.700 --> 00:32:58.860
you and other historians have
linked different examples

00:32:58.860 --> 00:33:01.890
of South Asian history to
current policy decisions.

00:33:01.890 --> 00:33:04.390
And I wonder if you can
speak to whether it comes

00:33:04.390 --> 00:33:09.390
to responding to COVID,
emerging from COVID

00:33:10.830 --> 00:33:14.310
and tackling this issue of climate

00:33:14.310 --> 00:33:16.240
and making these hard decisions.

00:33:16.240 --> 00:33:18.130
What are examples from India's past

00:33:18.130 --> 00:33:21.130
that might inform policy decisions
that need to be made now?

00:33:23.170 --> 00:33:24.890
- I mean, it increasingly seems to me

00:33:24.890 --> 00:33:28.440
that what we need in India is in a sense

00:33:28.440 --> 00:33:31.470
nothing short of the kind of
foundational conversations

00:33:31.470 --> 00:33:33.300
that were had in the
middle of the 20th century

00:33:33.300 --> 00:33:35.800
which many of my colleagues
have been working on.

00:33:35.800 --> 00:33:38.550
The sense of that
conversation in the mid '40s

00:33:38.550 --> 00:33:41.850
to the late '40s when the
fundamental questions we're asking

00:33:41.850 --> 00:33:43.750
what sort of politics,
what sort of society,

00:33:43.750 --> 00:33:46.770
what sort of economy is this going to be?

00:33:46.770 --> 00:33:49.240
I'm not optimistic that
political conditions

00:33:49.240 --> 00:33:52.490
are particularly propitious
for that kind of conversation,

00:33:52.490 --> 00:33:55.290
but I am struck by how much
of the unfinished business

00:33:55.290 --> 00:33:58.150
so that earlier moment
we are now dealing with.

00:33:58.150 --> 00:34:00.560
So that was a transformative moment.

00:34:00.560 --> 00:34:03.210
The universal franchise
was introduced in a country

00:34:03.210 --> 00:34:06.270
with profound levels of
social and economic inequality

00:34:06.270 --> 00:34:09.020
which many people at the time
said would be impossible.

00:34:10.300 --> 00:34:13.280
One of the things that did
not happen in the 1940s

00:34:13.280 --> 00:34:15.460
and there's a very, very
interesting graduate student

00:34:15.460 --> 00:34:16.810
at Harvard called Karen Kumba

00:34:16.810 --> 00:34:18.050
who's working on this at the moment.

00:34:18.050 --> 00:34:19.720
One of the things that did not happen

00:34:19.720 --> 00:34:24.290
was nothing was done to address
the chronic underinvestment

00:34:24.290 --> 00:34:26.410
in public health that
had been a characteristic

00:34:26.410 --> 00:34:28.370
of the colonial state in India.

00:34:28.370 --> 00:34:30.610
And throughout 20th century
and into this century,

00:34:30.610 --> 00:34:32.830
India spends a smaller
proportion of GDP on health

00:34:32.830 --> 00:34:34.950
than just about any country in the world.

00:34:34.950 --> 00:34:37.480
We're seeing the consequences of that now.

00:34:37.480 --> 00:34:40.070
And I mean, in some
sense, one of my puzzles

00:34:40.070 --> 00:34:42.160
and this has been true
for many, many years is

00:34:42.160 --> 00:34:45.160
why health has never really
been a political issue in India.

00:34:46.130 --> 00:34:48.030
At the state level,
yes, from time to time,

00:34:48.030 --> 00:34:51.050
but never it's not something
that wins elections.

00:34:51.050 --> 00:34:52.130
This is something that goes back

00:34:52.130 --> 00:34:53.350
to what Rory was saying earlier

00:34:53.350 --> 00:34:56.990
about the citizens of London
voting for their mayor

00:34:56.990 --> 00:34:59.860
were not perhaps willing
to spend even 15 pounds

00:34:59.860 --> 00:35:01.720
to address environmental questions.

00:35:01.720 --> 00:35:03.540
This is a much more puzzling question

00:35:03.540 --> 00:35:05.100
because health affects everybody,

00:35:05.100 --> 00:35:06.910
it affects the poorest most.

00:35:06.910 --> 00:35:10.780
And this has not been a topic
at least maybe until now

00:35:10.780 --> 00:35:13.350
for massive mobilization.

00:35:13.350 --> 00:35:16.070
The other connection that I
want to make in terms of India

00:35:16.070 --> 00:35:18.063
it goes to something
that Dieter was saying

00:35:18.063 --> 00:35:20.030
that is very interesting.

00:35:20.030 --> 00:35:22.230
So the Lancet found that in 2019,

00:35:22.230 --> 00:35:23.579
air pollution was responsible

00:35:23.579 --> 00:35:26.710
for 1.6 million deaths in India.

00:35:26.710 --> 00:35:29.360
The year before, that nearly as many.

00:35:29.360 --> 00:35:32.370
We're talking over two
or three years in India

00:35:32.370 --> 00:35:35.160
as many people die from air pollution

00:35:35.160 --> 00:35:38.170
as had died through this terrible pandemic

00:35:38.170 --> 00:35:39.883
on a global scale.

00:35:41.470 --> 00:35:43.440
I don't think it's an
exaggeration to say that

00:35:43.440 --> 00:35:45.380
air pollution has not received nearly

00:35:45.380 --> 00:35:49.360
as much media attention or
provoked as much anxiety

00:35:49.360 --> 00:35:51.300
or concern as this pandemic.

00:35:51.300 --> 00:35:53.470
So I think this goes to
what Dieter was saying

00:35:53.470 --> 00:35:56.360
about there are no walls we can build

00:35:56.360 --> 00:35:58.690
in terms of carbon emissions, for example

00:35:58.690 --> 00:36:01.933
unlike perhaps some of what
we've seen with the pandemic.

00:36:02.850 --> 00:36:03.970
So there are questions.

00:36:03.970 --> 00:36:07.410
I'm not sure there are
lessons from Indian history,

00:36:07.410 --> 00:36:09.520
but more sort of unfinished business

00:36:09.520 --> 00:36:12.350
that we need to sort of
return to the fundamental

00:36:12.350 --> 00:36:15.050
going to what Rory you were
saying, institutional questions,

00:36:15.050 --> 00:36:18.705
political questions about how
to prioritize public health.

00:36:18.705 --> 00:36:21.150
And from there to think
about public health

00:36:21.150 --> 00:36:21.990
in a way that goes

00:36:21.990 --> 00:36:24.731
beyond the emergency of COVID to the ways.

00:36:24.731 --> 00:36:26.810
The causes of bad condition, for example

00:36:26.810 --> 00:36:29.560
are far more aligned with
what we're talking about

00:36:29.560 --> 00:36:32.543
in relation to the climate
crisis and energy consumption.

00:36:34.430 --> 00:36:35.830
- Just very quickly say that.

00:36:35.830 --> 00:36:38.400
I mean, I think there's
something very important

00:36:38.400 --> 00:36:41.650
in Sunil's conversation about
that foundational moment

00:36:41.650 --> 00:36:43.280
in the 1940s and '50s in India

00:36:43.280 --> 00:36:46.430
because it's impossible to
get around the fact that

00:36:46.430 --> 00:36:49.563
there is an ethical dimension to this.

00:36:50.800 --> 00:36:54.390
Ultimately in order to
make our way through this

00:36:54.390 --> 00:36:59.380
is going to involve some
very, very dramatic element

00:36:59.380 --> 00:37:03.400
of sacrifice, transformation
and difficult choices.

00:37:03.400 --> 00:37:07.230
And somewhere hovering
underneath the surface

00:37:07.230 --> 00:37:09.500
of this conversation is the fact that

00:37:09.500 --> 00:37:12.070
actually what Dieter and
Rohini have explained

00:37:13.460 --> 00:37:15.900
is the impossibility

00:37:16.820 --> 00:37:20.050
of generating massive
increases in consumption

00:37:20.050 --> 00:37:22.130
and controlling climate change.

00:37:22.130 --> 00:37:23.743
Which means that in the end,

00:37:25.000 --> 00:37:28.020
our societies, our
politics have to find a way

00:37:28.020 --> 00:37:31.020
of talking about morality.

00:37:31.020 --> 00:37:34.750
Even as a politician,
I'm very, very conscious

00:37:34.750 --> 00:37:36.100
of how easy that is to say

00:37:36.100 --> 00:37:37.400
and how very difficult that is

00:37:37.400 --> 00:37:39.100
to pull into an election campaign.

00:37:40.390 --> 00:37:42.520
- Sorry, I'm jumping in a lot

00:37:42.520 --> 00:37:45.780
but let me just jump in on
one thing that Rory said.

00:37:45.780 --> 00:37:48.030
I think the implications
it also has for policy

00:37:48.030 --> 00:37:50.830
and I love to hear Dieter on this.

00:37:50.830 --> 00:37:52.670
We often have economists talk a lot

00:37:52.670 --> 00:37:54.750
about the most efficient policy.

00:37:54.750 --> 00:37:56.940
So for instance, we talk about carbon tax.

00:37:56.940 --> 00:37:58.790
I think Rory very clearly pointed out

00:37:58.790 --> 00:38:03.330
why something like a carbon
tax to fuel tax may not work.

00:38:03.330 --> 00:38:04.610
And so this suggests that

00:38:04.610 --> 00:38:06.350
even as we start designing policies,

00:38:06.350 --> 00:38:07.810
I think we need to think a lot more

00:38:07.810 --> 00:38:10.070
about distributional and fairness issues

00:38:10.070 --> 00:38:12.260
as part of this ethical issue

00:38:12.260 --> 00:38:15.920
which for instance may suggest
that sometimes rationing

00:38:15.920 --> 00:38:18.670
which we think of as quite
an inefficient policy

00:38:18.670 --> 00:38:22.850
may actually be one that
is perceived as fair,

00:38:22.850 --> 00:38:23.730
and we should get to it.

00:38:23.730 --> 00:38:26.070
So economists quite often talk

00:38:26.070 --> 00:38:29.850
about what they would
call second best policies.

00:38:29.850 --> 00:38:31.170
But they often think of second best

00:38:31.170 --> 00:38:33.390
because there is some market missing.

00:38:33.390 --> 00:38:34.936
I think we might need to think about

00:38:34.936 --> 00:38:37.610
that what are the best
environmental policies

00:38:37.610 --> 00:38:40.540
need to take into account
kind of issues of ethics

00:38:40.540 --> 00:38:42.310
and morality of distribution,

00:38:42.310 --> 00:38:43.550
and may look quite different

00:38:43.550 --> 00:38:46.050
from what I think a lot of
economists right now are pushing

00:38:46.050 --> 00:38:48.620
as sort of the
technocratically best policies

00:38:48.620 --> 00:38:49.843
like carbon tax.

00:38:51.410 --> 00:38:52.250
- Great points.

00:38:52.250 --> 00:38:54.140
Quickly I'll just say we have
a lot of questions coming in

00:38:54.140 --> 00:38:55.010
on exactly this

00:38:55.010 --> 00:38:57.200
like what policies should
we see moving forward?

00:38:57.200 --> 00:38:59.500
So this is great.
Go ahead, Dieter.

00:38:59.500 --> 00:39:04.240
- So I very much agree there's
ethics at the heart of this.

00:39:04.240 --> 00:39:07.470
And the way I kind of encapsulate that is

00:39:07.470 --> 00:39:09.640
you have to work out how future citizens

00:39:09.640 --> 00:39:11.810
are gonna have a seat at the table.

00:39:11.810 --> 00:39:13.810
And they're gonna have a seat at the table

00:39:13.810 --> 00:39:17.060
either because we haven't
made the sacrifices

00:39:17.060 --> 00:39:19.940
and their seat at the table
is to make the sacrifices

00:39:19.940 --> 00:39:21.970
or we're going to take into account

00:39:21.970 --> 00:39:23.180
their interests going forward.

00:39:23.180 --> 00:39:25.800
And my argument that we should ensure

00:39:25.800 --> 00:39:28.695
that each citizen
inherits a set of assets,

00:39:28.695 --> 00:39:31.600
natural assets at least
as good as they inherited

00:39:31.600 --> 00:39:33.630
is an ethical principle.

00:39:33.630 --> 00:39:35.160
But I also wanna pick up this point

00:39:35.160 --> 00:39:37.890
that Rory raised about
fuel taxes, et cetera

00:39:37.890 --> 00:39:40.760
and this link to carbon pricing, okay.

00:39:40.760 --> 00:39:41.950
So it's very interesting.

00:39:41.950 --> 00:39:42.783
As I understand it,

00:39:42.783 --> 00:39:45.770
Mr. Biden doesn't want to
put off fuel taxes either.

00:39:45.770 --> 00:39:49.050
It's not just the kind of UK thing

00:39:49.050 --> 00:39:51.410
or a particular political party thing.

00:39:51.410 --> 00:39:54.902
You tell the public, you know what?

00:39:54.902 --> 00:39:57.443
It's better than a free lunch.

00:39:58.340 --> 00:40:00.000
The climate change we're gonna solve it

00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:02.070
'cause isn't gonna cost much of GDP,

00:40:02.070 --> 00:40:02.937
it's all gonna happen,

00:40:02.937 --> 00:40:06.470
the cost of renewables are
cheaper than fossil fuels.

00:40:06.470 --> 00:40:09.270
It's like the 20 pound
loads lying on the ground

00:40:09.270 --> 00:40:10.537
and we just got to pick them up

00:40:10.537 --> 00:40:13.280
and it may actually be GDP positive, okay.

00:40:13.280 --> 00:40:17.330
And I said to myself, is this likely

00:40:17.330 --> 00:40:20.453
to which my answer is
absolutely no unfortunately.

00:40:21.710 --> 00:40:23.560
Is it likely that we can transfer

00:40:23.560 --> 00:40:26.020
from an overwhelmingly
carbon intensive economy

00:40:26.020 --> 00:40:29.950
to one that isn't carbon
intensive economy within 30 years?

00:40:29.950 --> 00:40:31.290
No.

00:40:31.290 --> 00:40:33.620
And therefore you get to this political

00:40:33.620 --> 00:40:37.040
what I call in inverted
commas lie which is

00:40:37.040 --> 00:40:39.060
tell them we're gonna do it,

00:40:39.060 --> 00:40:40.740
and tell them it isn't
gonna cost anything.

00:40:40.740 --> 00:40:44.020
Mr. Johnson put this in Biden summit

00:40:44.020 --> 00:40:45.800
a couple of weeks ago was saying

00:40:46.700 --> 00:40:48.270
that he was going to have cakeism,

00:40:48.270 --> 00:40:50.150
you could have your cake and eat it too.

00:40:50.150 --> 00:40:53.300
That's morally irresponsible, right?

00:40:53.300 --> 00:40:56.270
And we will have to increase the price.

00:40:56.270 --> 00:40:59.300
And we can either do it
of carbon either directly

00:40:59.300 --> 00:41:00.780
or having a carbon price.

00:41:00.780 --> 00:41:02.580
And we do in Europe, we do in the UK,

00:41:02.580 --> 00:41:04.000
actually most people have carbon prices

00:41:04.000 --> 00:41:06.700
'cause they have energy
taxes in some form or another

00:41:06.700 --> 00:41:07.900
or we can do it implicitly.

00:41:07.900 --> 00:41:10.660
We can say, we're not gonna
take the most efficient route

00:41:10.660 --> 00:41:12.560
because we don't like the politics of it.

00:41:12.560 --> 00:41:14.870
So let's find a route
that's less efficient.

00:41:14.870 --> 00:41:16.760
Well, the answer that is
gonna cost you even more

00:41:16.760 --> 00:41:18.920
than the first route did, okay.

00:41:18.920 --> 00:41:22.520
So I don't think carbon
pricing is impossible.

00:41:22.520 --> 00:41:24.010
It exists.

00:41:24.010 --> 00:41:27.410
Harmonizing energy taxes will
got us a long way towards it.

00:41:27.410 --> 00:41:30.110
But the bit of our carbon
pricing that I really want

00:41:30.110 --> 00:41:31.790
is the border adjustment

00:41:31.790 --> 00:41:35.120
so that we don't pretend
amongst the developed countries

00:41:35.120 --> 00:41:38.080
that when we reduce our
carbon territorial production

00:41:38.080 --> 00:41:41.550
to net zero, we're gonna
stop causing climate change.

00:41:41.550 --> 00:41:43.920
UK is 80% services.

00:41:43.920 --> 00:41:47.550
We have no large energy
intensive industries left.

00:41:47.550 --> 00:41:49.010
We pride ourselves as being

00:41:49.010 --> 00:41:51.770
the leading country in the
world in reducing emissions.

00:41:51.770 --> 00:41:54.070
This is the way to go guys, right.

00:41:54.070 --> 00:41:55.920
And actually the truth is

00:41:55.920 --> 00:41:59.840
our carbon consumption is
displacing our carbon production,

00:41:59.840 --> 00:42:01.640
it's very much higher,

00:42:01.640 --> 00:42:03.790
and that's because we're just not paying

00:42:03.790 --> 00:42:06.130
for our true carbon footprint.

00:42:06.130 --> 00:42:08.560
And paying for your true carbon footprint

00:42:08.560 --> 00:42:11.980
is at the base of any
moral or ethical approach

00:42:11.980 --> 00:42:15.280
to climate change here and anywhere else.

00:42:15.280 --> 00:42:17.630
And since we're doing
most of the consuming,

00:42:17.630 --> 00:42:20.820
and by the way we put up 80%
of the stuff in the atmosphere

00:42:20.820 --> 00:42:22.210
that's already up there,

00:42:22.210 --> 00:42:24.660
it's natural but it's our consumption

00:42:24.660 --> 00:42:25.930
the developed countries

00:42:25.930 --> 00:42:27.710
that has to come first to this equation

00:42:27.710 --> 00:42:29.950
and not to tackle that by telling people

00:42:29.950 --> 00:42:31.830
you don't have to have
a higher fuel price,

00:42:31.830 --> 00:42:34.730
you don't have to pay for higher gasoline,

00:42:34.730 --> 00:42:36.440
it's not gonna cost you anything.

00:42:36.440 --> 00:42:38.070
It's just delusional.

00:42:38.070 --> 00:42:39.790
And the consequence will be

00:42:39.790 --> 00:42:41.740
that we will have that climate change.

00:42:41.740 --> 00:42:43.770
And then back to the
early point of discussion,

00:42:43.770 --> 00:42:45.600
the people who really gonna suffer

00:42:45.600 --> 00:42:47.060
are in developing countries,

00:42:47.060 --> 00:42:50.450
some in developed countries too.

00:42:50.450 --> 00:42:51.283
But remember,

00:42:52.370 --> 00:42:55.060
as witnessed in the Indian
case with COVID at the moment

00:42:55.060 --> 00:42:57.680
compared with some of
the developing countries,

00:42:57.680 --> 00:43:00.380
even the less well off in
the developed countries

00:43:00.380 --> 00:43:02.380
have some kind of safety net

00:43:02.380 --> 00:43:04.020
which unfortunately doesn't exist

00:43:04.020 --> 00:43:06.450
in most of the world's population.

00:43:06.450 --> 00:43:07.530
- Very true.

00:43:07.530 --> 00:43:09.740
I wanna bring in some
questions from the audience

00:43:09.740 --> 00:43:11.620
and we have some great ones coming in.

00:43:11.620 --> 00:43:12.570
And I'd remind you all,

00:43:12.570 --> 00:43:14.860
please use the Q&amp;A function on Zoom

00:43:14.860 --> 00:43:17.110
if you're joining us on
Zoom to submit those.

00:43:18.000 --> 00:43:19.860
There is a question related to technology.

00:43:19.860 --> 00:43:24.170
And I think we kind of started
with technology as a given.

00:43:24.170 --> 00:43:26.000
Like we have the technologies.

00:43:26.000 --> 00:43:28.700
And so it's about
persuasion and incentives

00:43:28.700 --> 00:43:32.340
and hard choices, but a
question on that technology.

00:43:32.340 --> 00:43:36.120
So what global adaptation
to green energy only happen

00:43:36.120 --> 00:43:38.910
when cost-effective and
dependable technologies

00:43:38.910 --> 00:43:41.000
make that adaptation desirable

00:43:41.000 --> 00:43:43.820
without being forced
by government policies?

00:43:43.820 --> 00:43:47.740
In that regard, isn't research
into critical obstacles

00:43:47.740 --> 00:43:49.970
like energy storage and safe nuclear

00:43:49.970 --> 00:43:51.830
the most important thing to focus on now.

00:43:51.830 --> 00:43:53.230
So I appreciate the question

00:43:53.230 --> 00:43:54.790
because again we kind of just plowed

00:43:54.790 --> 00:43:55.623
through the technology point

00:43:55.623 --> 00:44:00.200
and this forces us to
return to that question.

00:44:00.200 --> 00:44:02.460
What more do we need
on the technology front

00:44:02.460 --> 00:44:04.920
even as we are tackling
all these other issues

00:44:04.920 --> 00:44:09.530
in terms of incentives
and ethics and persuasion?

00:44:10.410 --> 00:44:12.650
Any thoughts on technology please?

00:44:12.650 --> 00:44:14.670
- Can I have the go?
- [Catherine] Please.

00:44:14.670 --> 00:44:17.330
- And I said technology
right at the beginning.

00:44:17.330 --> 00:44:19.480
The reason why economic growth as possible

00:44:19.480 --> 00:44:21.920
is because technology is pushing out.

00:44:21.920 --> 00:44:25.660
And aside from Robert Barrow's book,

00:44:25.660 --> 00:44:29.510
actually the evidence is the
speed of technical progress

00:44:29.510 --> 00:44:31.450
has never been greater.

00:44:31.450 --> 00:44:33.950
And this really matters for climate change

00:44:33.950 --> 00:44:37.550
not just for the emissions
for the sequestration too.

00:44:37.550 --> 00:44:42.550
So 25% of global emissions at
least come from agriculture,

00:44:42.620 --> 00:44:46.640
soil on average has four times
the carbon of the atmosphere.

00:44:46.640 --> 00:44:49.180
That's the biggest stock
of carbon is below our feet

00:44:49.180 --> 00:44:52.510
and agriculture rips that out
pretty quickly especially,

00:44:52.510 --> 00:44:55.070
unfortunately in some of
the developing countries.

00:44:55.070 --> 00:44:57.280
If you look at what's
going on in genetics,

00:44:57.280 --> 00:44:59.290
if you look at what's
going on in gene editing,

00:44:59.290 --> 00:45:00.840
if you look at the transformation

00:45:00.840 --> 00:45:04.090
that digital information
has created for agriculture,

00:45:04.090 --> 00:45:06.110
that's a revolution it's making.

00:45:06.110 --> 00:45:09.150
When it comes to the energy side,

00:45:09.150 --> 00:45:12.127
there is of course great
technical progress.

00:45:12.127 --> 00:45:17.127
The problem is that almost
all the hope and aspirations

00:45:17.620 --> 00:45:21.210
are placed on two de-centralized

00:45:21.210 --> 00:45:25.650
low energy density technologies
which cannot in themselves

00:45:25.650 --> 00:45:27.540
produce the kind of power

00:45:27.540 --> 00:45:30.540
required for the economy of the
world as it is at the moment

00:45:30.540 --> 00:45:31.650
net loan what's coming.

00:45:31.650 --> 00:45:33.040
I'm talking about wind and solar.

00:45:33.040 --> 00:45:35.590
Solar by the way is much
more promising than wind.

00:45:35.590 --> 00:45:38.700
You need a lot of wind
turbines and a lot of backup

00:45:38.700 --> 00:45:40.710
and a lot of batteries
and a lot of storage

00:45:40.710 --> 00:45:42.360
and all that's important.

00:45:42.360 --> 00:45:44.750
But I think it's delusional to think

00:45:44.750 --> 00:45:47.470
that we're going to solve climate change

00:45:47.470 --> 00:45:50.510
with wind turbines and solar panels alone.

00:45:50.510 --> 00:45:52.020
We need quite a lot more.

00:45:52.020 --> 00:45:55.190
That requires an honest
discussion about technology.

00:45:55.190 --> 00:45:57.400
First of all, the enormous resources

00:45:57.400 --> 00:46:00.240
that ought to go into genuine R and D

00:46:00.240 --> 00:46:04.828
which is a fantastically
on average good investment

00:46:04.828 --> 00:46:05.830
for publics to make

00:46:05.830 --> 00:46:09.390
even if the chances of each
one's accedence is quite small.

00:46:09.390 --> 00:46:10.970
And we have to make some choices

00:46:10.970 --> 00:46:13.290
about whether we're really
prepared to do this.

00:46:13.290 --> 00:46:17.210
And I'm not advocating we
should without big scale energy

00:46:17.210 --> 00:46:18.043
as well as small scale

00:46:18.043 --> 00:46:21.660
and that means hydrogen,
nuclear and things of that sort.

00:46:21.660 --> 00:46:23.130
And we're not there yet,

00:46:23.130 --> 00:46:25.590
but we will not crack climate change

00:46:25.590 --> 00:46:28.940
unless we have both the technology

00:46:28.940 --> 00:46:31.180
and the adjustment of
the consumption frame.

00:46:31.180 --> 00:46:33.180
Neither of them will do it on their own.

00:46:35.600 --> 00:46:37.020
- Thank you, and thanks
for that great question.

00:46:37.020 --> 00:46:39.880
Any other panelists want
to jump in on technology?

00:46:39.880 --> 00:46:41.020
Otherwise we have other questions coming.

00:46:41.020 --> 00:46:42.230
Go ahead, Rohini.

00:46:42.230 --> 00:46:43.500
- I just have one quick thing

00:46:43.500 --> 00:46:48.500
which it reminds me a
bit of sort of the focus

00:46:49.601 --> 00:46:51.330
in the West coast, for instance,

00:46:51.330 --> 00:46:55.270
in a lot of tech driven
for development policy.

00:46:55.270 --> 00:46:57.183
I think 10 years ago this was the thing

00:46:57.183 --> 00:47:01.650
that we were going to just
send money on mobile phones

00:47:01.650 --> 00:47:03.440
and that was going to
solve all the problems

00:47:03.440 --> 00:47:04.640
of redistribution.

00:47:04.640 --> 00:47:06.430
I think one thing we discovered is

00:47:06.430 --> 00:47:07.690
it didn't get the whole way there

00:47:07.690 --> 00:47:10.750
not because mobile phones
are not an amazing good

00:47:10.750 --> 00:47:13.170
or that sending money didn't help people.

00:47:13.170 --> 00:47:16.400
But there were countries that
the regulatory agencies said

00:47:16.400 --> 00:47:18.490
they didn't want to allow for mobile money

00:47:18.490 --> 00:47:20.720
because they were worried about
risks to the banking system

00:47:20.720 --> 00:47:21.553
and so on.

00:47:21.553 --> 00:47:23.200
So I think my only note of caution

00:47:23.200 --> 00:47:24.260
is I completely agree with Dieter

00:47:24.260 --> 00:47:27.140
that we need, I think significant R and D.

00:47:27.140 --> 00:47:28.660
But I think we shouldn't do it

00:47:28.660 --> 00:47:29.790
at the expense of thinking that

00:47:29.790 --> 00:47:32.300
that technology you need to think about

00:47:32.300 --> 00:47:34.760
how are regulators going to react to it?

00:47:34.760 --> 00:47:36.080
And they're going to be
willing to put it in?

00:47:36.080 --> 00:47:38.290
And even technology can cause trade-offs

00:47:38.290 --> 00:47:40.600
between short-term as already described

00:47:40.600 --> 00:47:43.070
in a poverty reduction measures
and longer run returns.

00:47:43.070 --> 00:47:45.010
You may have a great technology

00:47:45.010 --> 00:47:47.490
but what it might ask the
country to do in the short-term

00:47:47.490 --> 00:47:49.774
may make it politically very attractable.

00:47:49.774 --> 00:47:52.390
And I think if we could start
having those conversations

00:47:52.390 --> 00:47:53.580
or even think about them

00:47:53.580 --> 00:47:56.480
at the time when you're
financing technology,

00:47:56.480 --> 00:47:57.590
I think we could learn a lot

00:47:57.590 --> 00:48:00.620
from at least what hasn't
worked up in development policy

00:48:00.620 --> 00:48:02.843
despite great technological advances.

00:48:03.930 --> 00:48:05.300
- There's another question here

00:48:05.300 --> 00:48:06.840
related to the private sector.

00:48:06.840 --> 00:48:08.250
And I'm loving these questions

00:48:08.250 --> 00:48:10.670
because they're all very action oriented,

00:48:10.670 --> 00:48:12.640
the role of technology, the
role of the private sector,

00:48:12.640 --> 00:48:15.210
and I'll just preview in a
moment the role of universities.

00:48:15.210 --> 00:48:16.090
There's a question around that

00:48:16.090 --> 00:48:17.470
which I think would be fascinating

00:48:17.470 --> 00:48:18.690
for this group to take on.

00:48:18.690 --> 00:48:20.370
But let's talk about the private sector.

00:48:20.370 --> 00:48:23.350
So the question is almost
a thousand companies

00:48:23.350 --> 00:48:26.310
have signed on to the
science-based targets initiative

00:48:26.310 --> 00:48:28.280
pledging to set emissions
reduction targets

00:48:28.280 --> 00:48:30.450
consistent with the Paris trajectory

00:48:30.450 --> 00:48:32.440
along with major companies setting

00:48:32.440 --> 00:48:34.400
similar carbon neutrality goals.

00:48:34.400 --> 00:48:37.390
So in what ways can
these ambitious targets

00:48:37.390 --> 00:48:39.460
in private enterprise help support

00:48:39.460 --> 00:48:42.730
and even spur governments to
step up their own commitments?

00:48:42.730 --> 00:48:44.958
Can the private sector help lead the way?

00:48:44.958 --> 00:48:47.520
Does it play a meaningful role?

00:48:47.520 --> 00:48:48.383
Thoughts on that.

00:48:51.814 --> 00:48:53.067
Rohini.
- Yeah, I can jump in.

00:48:53.067 --> 00:48:54.990
I mean, I think it's great.

00:48:54.990 --> 00:48:56.920
I think it's great to
see voluntary efforts

00:48:56.920 --> 00:48:59.930
but I think we should think
about kind of the hard questions

00:48:59.930 --> 00:49:00.763
at the heart of this.

00:49:00.763 --> 00:49:02.580
But a lot of companies

00:49:02.580 --> 00:49:04.653
really taking climate change seriously

00:49:05.790 --> 00:49:07.180
is about losing money, right?

00:49:07.180 --> 00:49:09.380
If you are a fossil fuel company,

00:49:09.380 --> 00:49:12.190
there is no way that this is
actually good news for you.

00:49:12.190 --> 00:49:13.139
So we have to recognize that

00:49:13.139 --> 00:49:16.450
while voluntary actions
will get us some of the way,

00:49:16.450 --> 00:49:18.610
it's not going to be the only way

00:49:18.610 --> 00:49:20.320
in which private sector needs to be moved.

00:49:20.320 --> 00:49:24.120
Sometimes you do need the state
to come in as the regulator

00:49:24.120 --> 00:49:26.230
pushing private companies to take actions

00:49:26.230 --> 00:49:27.373
that they won't want to.

00:49:28.527 --> 00:49:31.550
- I think one other thing
to bear in mind also that

00:49:31.550 --> 00:49:33.410
it's very easy for companies to announce

00:49:33.410 --> 00:49:36.260
they're going to be net
zero by a certain date.

00:49:36.260 --> 00:49:38.400
It's quite another when
you look beyond the bonnet

00:49:38.400 --> 00:49:40.600
what they actually think
they're going to do.

00:49:40.600 --> 00:49:41.520
And there are two parts of that.

00:49:41.520 --> 00:49:43.690
First of all, remember
companies don't exist

00:49:43.690 --> 00:49:45.020
for the fun of being companies.

00:49:45.020 --> 00:49:47.530
They exist to sell stuff to you and me.

00:49:47.530 --> 00:49:51.640
So when companies pollute, the
customer who buys the product

00:49:51.640 --> 00:49:55.560
is actually the ultimate
polluter and this is facilitated.

00:49:55.560 --> 00:49:57.430
So we castigate go companies,

00:49:57.430 --> 00:50:00.320
but we go and fill up the
car with petrol or diesel.

00:50:00.320 --> 00:50:01.470
You can't have it both ways,

00:50:01.470 --> 00:50:02.770
you've got to put it in the frame.

00:50:02.770 --> 00:50:05.290
But the bit I would just
want to illustrate is

00:50:05.290 --> 00:50:06.123
a lot of companies say,

00:50:06.123 --> 00:50:09.588
well, we're investing in
trying to get emissions

00:50:09.588 --> 00:50:11.540
out of our production plan.

00:50:11.540 --> 00:50:14.570
But you know what, we've
done a load of offsets.

00:50:14.570 --> 00:50:17.120
And you really have to look very hard

00:50:17.120 --> 00:50:18.910
at what constitutes an offset.

00:50:18.910 --> 00:50:21.310
And it looks like nearly half

00:50:21.310 --> 00:50:24.880
of what companies are claiming
to be their net zero path

00:50:26.268 --> 00:50:29.510
is actually just buying
into renewables projects

00:50:29.510 --> 00:50:32.360
many of which would have to happen anyway.

00:50:32.360 --> 00:50:33.450
You have to be very careful,

00:50:33.450 --> 00:50:36.980
this isn't the religious
version of buying indulgences.

00:50:36.980 --> 00:50:39.380
Let me go on doing what I'm doing

00:50:39.380 --> 00:50:42.850
but I'll play a side payment
to try to explicate myself.

00:50:42.850 --> 00:50:45.370
And that doesn't in any
way detract from the need

00:50:45.370 --> 00:50:47.870
for companies to take
this stuff seriously.

00:50:47.870 --> 00:50:49.100
But I think this cascade

00:50:49.100 --> 00:50:51.587
of people declaring
themselves net zero needs

00:50:51.587 --> 00:50:56.333
just a little bit of cautious
and critical examination.

00:50:57.610 --> 00:51:00.090
- Agreed, and I throw out as a journalist,

00:51:00.090 --> 00:51:01.043
that journalism plays an important role.

00:51:01.043 --> 00:51:03.650
They're not just featuring
the announcements,

00:51:03.650 --> 00:51:06.190
but asking what's actually happening.

00:51:06.190 --> 00:51:08.430
Rory, did you wanna jump
in on this question?

00:51:08.430 --> 00:51:09.263
- Yeah.

00:51:10.450 --> 00:51:14.264
I think that just to sort of step back

00:51:14.264 --> 00:51:16.300
the fundamentals that
we keep coming back to

00:51:16.300 --> 00:51:19.630
which I think everybody
keeps emphasizing is that

00:51:19.630 --> 00:51:23.557
if the scale of the problem is
as large as we're putting out

00:51:23.557 --> 00:51:27.700
and the forms of sacrifice
required are that extreme,

00:51:27.700 --> 00:51:29.360
and it's extremely unlikely

00:51:29.360 --> 00:51:32.120
that you're gonna be able to do this

00:51:32.120 --> 00:51:33.800
without a very significant impact

00:51:33.800 --> 00:51:35.630
on growth consumption, et cetera,

00:51:35.630 --> 00:51:38.930
it's intuitively very, very unlikely

00:51:38.930 --> 00:51:41.720
that private companies are
gonna be able to lead this

00:51:41.720 --> 00:51:43.620
for all the reasons that each revenue.

00:51:45.570 --> 00:51:48.430
- Thank you, I wanna actually
directly asked this question

00:51:48.430 --> 00:51:53.090
around universities especially
because we are wrapping up

00:51:53.090 --> 00:51:56.680
for this academic year the
Yale Development Dialogues.

00:51:56.680 --> 00:51:58.150
So the question is,

00:51:58.150 --> 00:52:00.727
what is the role of our
universities in this whole process

00:52:00.727 --> 00:52:05.727
as knowledge and as
dialogue with the world?

00:52:05.870 --> 00:52:08.718
So I think that's a good point.

00:52:08.718 --> 00:52:10.700
Why are we having this conversation?

00:52:10.700 --> 00:52:13.670
And what more can universities do

00:52:13.670 --> 00:52:16.700
in terms of helping to
carve the path forward?

00:52:16.700 --> 00:52:17.770
Rory, did you wanna jump in?

00:52:17.770 --> 00:52:18.910
- Yeah, well, let me comment quickly

00:52:18.910 --> 00:52:20.960
as the one person who
really isn't entitled

00:52:20.960 --> 00:52:21.910
to speak about this at all

00:52:21.910 --> 00:52:24.030
given that I've got three
distinguished professors

00:52:24.030 --> 00:52:25.280
on the call.

00:52:25.280 --> 00:52:27.990
But for me as somebody
who was a politician

00:52:27.990 --> 00:52:31.430
until very recently and
I'm now at a university,

00:52:31.430 --> 00:52:34.760
it is unbelievably refreshing and valuable

00:52:34.760 --> 00:52:36.670
to be able to have these
types of conversation.

00:52:36.670 --> 00:52:38.760
I mean, the last 45 minutes conversation

00:52:38.760 --> 00:52:42.790
simply doesn't happen in
politics for 10,000 reasons

00:52:42.790 --> 00:52:44.750
to do with the way that elections work,

00:52:44.750 --> 00:52:47.220
the attention deficit of politicians,

00:52:47.220 --> 00:52:48.870
the general mode you're thinking.

00:52:50.370 --> 00:52:52.700
If one were able to take simply

00:52:52.700 --> 00:52:54.530
the last 45 minutes of conversation

00:52:54.530 --> 00:52:57.830
and actually embed it
in the way that Biden

00:52:57.830 --> 00:53:02.300
or Boris Johnson or Macron
thinks about the world

00:53:02.300 --> 00:53:05.023
but it makes the most
extraordinary difference.

00:53:06.690 --> 00:53:08.500
That's why for me at least

00:53:08.500 --> 00:53:10.100
universities are incredibly useful

00:53:10.100 --> 00:53:13.330
because it allows you to say
things which are totally brutal

00:53:13.330 --> 00:53:15.760
because I can also show you
as an ex working politician

00:53:15.760 --> 00:53:17.620
at least six things that everybody said

00:53:17.620 --> 00:53:19.660
which would ruin their political careers

00:53:19.660 --> 00:53:20.810
in the last 45 minutes.

00:53:22.300 --> 00:53:23.850
- I love it, thank you, Rory.

00:53:23.850 --> 00:53:26.280
Do others want to jump in on
this, the role of universities?

00:53:26.280 --> 00:53:27.770
I guess a question for me is

00:53:27.770 --> 00:53:30.950
how do we pull these
kinds of conversations

00:53:30.950 --> 00:53:32.400
into the halls of government

00:53:32.400 --> 00:53:34.980
into the heads of policy makers

00:53:34.980 --> 00:53:36.960
as they're navigating
these hard decisions?

00:53:36.960 --> 00:53:38.690
- I goes both ways round, isn't it?

00:53:38.690 --> 00:53:40.560
I mean, give you an example,

00:53:40.560 --> 00:53:43.253
just a few hundred yards from here,

00:53:44.110 --> 00:53:49.110
a group of scientists got
hold of the genetic frame map

00:53:49.350 --> 00:53:51.310
of the coronavirus,

00:53:51.310 --> 00:53:54.203
and within just over a week
had invented a vaccine.

00:53:55.410 --> 00:53:57.210
You don't get that anywhere else

00:53:57.210 --> 00:54:00.740
because you don't get that
independent long-term research.

00:54:00.740 --> 00:54:03.500
Do you know the ultimate
driver of economic growth

00:54:03.500 --> 00:54:06.900
and actually the ultimate driver of hope

00:54:06.900 --> 00:54:10.130
to address climate change is ideas.

00:54:10.130 --> 00:54:14.260
And ideas are not always
produced in universities,

00:54:14.260 --> 00:54:16.360
but it's damn hard to drive them forward

00:54:16.360 --> 00:54:18.380
without universities

00:54:18.380 --> 00:54:22.360
and without engaging
with the next generation

00:54:22.360 --> 00:54:24.130
who are gonna inherit this planet

00:54:24.130 --> 00:54:27.800
and hopefully with a few
more natural capital assets

00:54:27.800 --> 00:54:30.000
that we're currently
on course to lead them.

00:54:31.120 --> 00:54:33.270
- Sunil, go ahead.
- A few things.

00:54:33.270 --> 00:54:34.630
In terms of that interface

00:54:34.630 --> 00:54:39.110
between these kinds of discussions
and the world of policy,

00:54:39.110 --> 00:54:42.520
my main source of optimism
in general is our students.

00:54:42.520 --> 00:54:44.170
I mean, I think they are the main conduit

00:54:44.170 --> 00:54:45.110
between the kinds of conversations

00:54:45.110 --> 00:54:47.730
that we can have in the
classroom and many of them

00:54:47.730 --> 00:54:51.240
will go on to be
journalists and politicians

00:54:51.240 --> 00:54:52.410
and policymakers.

00:54:52.410 --> 00:54:55.790
And so that for me remains the
single most important thing

00:54:55.790 --> 00:54:58.310
that universities do is the teaching

00:54:58.310 --> 00:54:59.947
as well as research, of course.

00:54:59.947 --> 00:55:01.650
As someone in the humanities,

00:55:01.650 --> 00:55:03.260
I've been involved with
a lot of conversations

00:55:03.260 --> 00:55:04.320
with so many of my colleagues

00:55:04.320 --> 00:55:07.020
about how we fold climate change

00:55:08.150 --> 00:55:10.300
completely into what we do.

00:55:10.300 --> 00:55:13.530
Not as a sort of just
another topic that we study,

00:55:13.530 --> 00:55:15.973
but how do we rethink the humanities.

00:55:16.870 --> 00:55:18.203
With the climate crisis

00:55:18.203 --> 00:55:20.610
sort of very foremost
of our consciousness,

00:55:20.610 --> 00:55:21.880
I think there are a lot of conversations

00:55:21.880 --> 00:55:23.160
happening around that.

00:55:23.160 --> 00:55:24.160
But the last thing I want to say

00:55:24.160 --> 00:55:26.070
about universities and climate change

00:55:26.070 --> 00:55:27.180
picking up on many things

00:55:27.180 --> 00:55:28.230
that have been said in this conversation

00:55:28.230 --> 00:55:30.330
is we need some honesty about that too.

00:55:30.330 --> 00:55:32.625
I'm not sure that universities

00:55:32.625 --> 00:55:35.130
pay for that carbon footprints.

00:55:35.130 --> 00:55:37.800
I'm not sure particularly universities

00:55:37.800 --> 00:55:41.190
like Yale and others with
very large endowments

00:55:41.190 --> 00:55:44.240
have fully thought through
the responsibilities

00:55:44.240 --> 00:55:46.363
on that side of things.

00:55:47.840 --> 00:55:48.673
- Thank you, Sunil.

00:55:48.673 --> 00:55:50.090
And we just have a few minutes left.

00:55:50.090 --> 00:55:51.720
So I wanna close with one question

00:55:51.720 --> 00:55:53.800
and I'm sorry for those great questions

00:55:53.800 --> 00:55:55.490
we weren't able to get to.

00:55:55.490 --> 00:55:56.600
A lot of good questions coming in.

00:55:56.600 --> 00:55:59.820
But COP26 has come up a few times.

00:55:59.820 --> 00:56:04.140
And some of our panelists
have discussed that

00:56:05.070 --> 00:56:08.530
we have these meetings,
commitments are made,

00:56:08.530 --> 00:56:12.350
and yet as Dieter mentioned
earlier, emissions continue.

00:56:12.350 --> 00:56:17.330
Like we start to lose hope in the process.

00:56:17.330 --> 00:56:20.070
So with COP26 around the corner

00:56:20.070 --> 00:56:22.703
taking place in the context of COVID-19,

00:56:23.650 --> 00:56:26.150
we only have about 30 seconds
or so from each of you.

00:56:26.150 --> 00:56:29.430
But what's one thing
you want to see happen.

00:56:29.430 --> 00:56:32.270
What could rich and poor countries do

00:56:32.270 --> 00:56:33.770
between now and November?

00:56:33.770 --> 00:56:35.240
Or I should broaden it
since we talked about it,

00:56:35.240 --> 00:56:37.390
it's not just a matter of geography.

00:56:37.390 --> 00:56:40.530
What should citizens do
between now and November?

00:56:40.530 --> 00:56:42.470
Whether it's citizens,
whether it's policymakers,

00:56:42.470 --> 00:56:45.180
what's one thing you want to
see to improve the prospects

00:56:45.180 --> 00:56:48.110
for real progress to be made at COP26.

00:56:48.110 --> 00:56:50.180
And maybe we'll start with our co-hosts

00:56:50.180 --> 00:56:52.060
and then conclude with
Dieter, if that's all right.

00:56:52.060 --> 00:56:54.240
Rohini can I begin with you?

00:56:54.240 --> 00:56:55.151
- Sure.

00:56:55.151 --> 00:56:58.540
This is also trying to just
pick up on one of the questions

00:56:59.990 --> 00:57:02.120
in the Q&amp;A.

00:57:02.120 --> 00:57:03.530
I think what can we didn't touch upon

00:57:03.530 --> 00:57:04.890
that I think is gonna be critical

00:57:04.890 --> 00:57:06.750
that we start seeing honest conversations

00:57:06.750 --> 00:57:09.850
on is the area of carbon
capture and storage.

00:57:09.850 --> 00:57:12.000
I think most models of growth right now

00:57:12.000 --> 00:57:14.860
that try to be sustainable
are built on large amounts

00:57:14.860 --> 00:57:17.510
of sequestration of CCS happening.

00:57:17.510 --> 00:57:19.190
We don't understand anything about

00:57:19.190 --> 00:57:21.380
I think of the economics of it,

00:57:21.380 --> 00:57:23.950
how it's going to be achieved,
how will this happen?

00:57:23.950 --> 00:57:25.990
A concern is in something like COP26

00:57:25.990 --> 00:57:28.390
that can become the big
thing that everyone says.

00:57:28.390 --> 00:57:31.350
We will do a lot more carbon
capture and sequestration

00:57:31.350 --> 00:57:33.250
but this is not realistic at all.

00:57:33.250 --> 00:57:36.340
So I think having kind of more
honest conversations on that

00:57:36.340 --> 00:57:37.490
would be very valuable.

00:57:38.970 --> 00:57:42.480
- Thank you, I'll go to Rory
next, if that's all right.

00:57:42.480 --> 00:57:44.959
- I think COP26 is really the opportunity

00:57:44.959 --> 00:57:47.020
for politicians to do something

00:57:47.020 --> 00:57:49.740
that politicians particularly
in an age of populism

00:57:49.740 --> 00:57:51.210
find very difficult

00:57:51.210 --> 00:57:54.020
which is to get away from cakeism,

00:57:54.020 --> 00:57:57.200
get away from having all
cake and being able to eat it

00:57:57.200 --> 00:57:59.460
and focusing instead on
detail uncomfortable truths.

00:57:59.460 --> 00:58:02.160
And if there's one message
that you want to try to get

00:58:03.210 --> 00:58:06.270
it is what we will have to
do is going to have an impact

00:58:06.270 --> 00:58:08.050
on our growth and assumption.

00:58:08.050 --> 00:58:11.090
If you can just get that
single message through

00:58:11.090 --> 00:58:13.410
and suggest to people
that there will be pain

00:58:13.410 --> 00:58:14.860
and there will be sacrifice involved,

00:58:14.860 --> 00:58:16.630
that's probably the
most important beginning

00:58:16.630 --> 00:58:18.490
for this conversation.

00:58:18.490 --> 00:58:20.467
- Great point, thank you.
Sunil.

00:58:20.467 --> 00:58:22.250
- And honest conversation seemed to be

00:58:22.250 --> 00:58:23.450
the theme of our conversation now.

00:58:23.450 --> 00:58:25.763
And I think that is what is needed.

00:58:27.120 --> 00:58:29.060
As a humanist, I think narrative matters

00:58:29.060 --> 00:58:31.050
and what I would like to see citizens

00:58:31.050 --> 00:58:33.120
and scholars and activists do

00:58:33.120 --> 00:58:35.730
is to find a way of linking
what we've all been through

00:58:35.730 --> 00:58:37.290
with this coronavirus pandemic

00:58:37.290 --> 00:58:39.830
with a much longer term future.

00:58:39.830 --> 00:58:42.670
And the fact that the climate
crisis and the coronavirus

00:58:42.670 --> 00:58:45.180
crisis are connected in many, many ways.

00:58:45.180 --> 00:58:48.900
And we've been deeply conscious
of one of these crises

00:58:48.900 --> 00:58:52.410
and willfully ignoring the
other for the most part.

00:58:52.410 --> 00:58:55.900
So I would like to see
stories, narratives,

00:58:55.900 --> 00:58:57.180
ways of linking these things

00:58:57.180 --> 00:58:59.510
and ways that are meaningful to people.

00:58:59.510 --> 00:59:01.140
- Absolutely agree with you.

00:59:01.140 --> 00:59:02.460
Dieter, we'll close with your thoughts

00:59:02.460 --> 00:59:05.160
on what needs to happen
between now and COP26

00:59:05.160 --> 00:59:08.130
for this to really make
a meaningful difference.

00:59:08.130 --> 00:59:11.870
- Well, I think that whereas
the politicians all queuing up

00:59:11.870 --> 00:59:13.980
to make announcements about numbers

00:59:13.980 --> 00:59:15.630
many of which they don't understand

00:59:15.630 --> 00:59:17.460
and I'm not against that process,

00:59:17.460 --> 00:59:20.540
I would like the concentration
to be shining the torch

00:59:20.540 --> 00:59:22.570
on what's really going on.

00:59:22.570 --> 00:59:25.980
I would like a reconnection
between natural capital

00:59:25.980 --> 00:59:27.770
biodiversity and climate change.

00:59:27.770 --> 00:59:30.700
This is a global environmental crisis,

00:59:30.700 --> 00:59:32.870
not just a climate one.

00:59:32.870 --> 00:59:35.260
I would like the accounting done properly

00:59:35.260 --> 00:59:38.750
so we measure who is really
responsible for carbon

00:59:38.750 --> 00:59:40.410
and obviously like carbon pricing

00:59:40.410 --> 00:59:42.950
'cause I want the polluters to pay.

00:59:42.950 --> 00:59:46.470
And finally, I think what
I'd like individuals to do,

00:59:46.470 --> 00:59:49.660
citizens to do is write
their own carbon diary

00:59:49.660 --> 00:59:51.930
and look very hard into their own lives

00:59:51.930 --> 00:59:55.390
and write down how much during
the average day they think

00:59:55.390 --> 00:59:57.760
is the carbon emissions
that they are responsible

00:59:57.760 --> 00:59:59.250
through their consumption.

00:59:59.250 --> 01:00:02.100
And ask themselves how in 30 years time

01:00:02.100 --> 01:00:04.960
they're gonna write the same
carbon diary as a citizen

01:00:04.960 --> 01:00:06.460
with no carbon in it.

01:00:06.460 --> 01:00:09.180
Then they'll get a
measure of the sheer scale

01:00:09.180 --> 01:00:12.170
of the task in front of
us which we must address

01:00:12.170 --> 01:00:15.623
because if we don't, the
consequences will address us.

01:00:17.260 --> 01:00:20.510
- Well, put in a powerful call
to action to close us out.

01:00:20.510 --> 01:00:22.470
So it's my task now to wrap up.

01:00:22.470 --> 01:00:24.020
This is a fascinating conversation,

01:00:24.020 --> 01:00:25.450
thank you to all of our panelists.

01:00:25.450 --> 01:00:28.300
And as our 2020, 2021 season

01:00:28.300 --> 01:00:30.410
of the Yale Development
Dialogues comes to an end,

01:00:30.410 --> 01:00:31.860
we're beginning to plan our series

01:00:31.860 --> 01:00:33.330
for the next academic year.

01:00:33.330 --> 01:00:34.940
And we would really love all of your input

01:00:34.940 --> 01:00:36.630
on the topics we should cover.

